1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,464
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:02,464 --> 00:00:03,937
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:03,937 --> 00:00:07,374
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:15,000
SARA BEERY: So today, we're going

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,760
to be doing our last of this mini series

6
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:21,000
on machine learning architectures or deep learning

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,560
architectures.

8
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:26,320
So previously, we talked about CNNs, GNNs, things

9
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:27,420
like transformers.

10
00:00:27,420 --> 00:00:29,800
And today, we're going to be introducing

11
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:33,300
the concepts of memory and sequence modeling.

12
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:36,440
And we have seen different types of ways to model sequences

13
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:36,980
in the past.

14
00:00:36,980 --> 00:00:41,320
We talked about 3D CNNs that have time or transformers that

15
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:42,960
have a sense of time.

16
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:46,560
But today, we're going to be introducing a new class of types

17
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:50,760
of models that handle memory and sequences in a different way.

18
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:53,400
And this will be the first class of architectures

19
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:55,720
that we talk about that's actually,

20
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:57,800
quote, unquote, Turing complete.

21
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:00,580
So this is actually the most general framework

22
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:04,099
of the architectures that we're going to be introducing.

23
00:01:04,099 --> 00:01:07,620
So we'll first motivate again why

24
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:10,780
we think we might want to model sequences, model

25
00:01:10,780 --> 00:01:13,780
something like time or order.

26
00:01:13,780 --> 00:01:17,900
And then we'll talk about how we might use CNNs for sequences.

27
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:20,260
We'll introduce the concept of an RNN,

28
00:01:20,260 --> 00:01:22,460
or Recurrent Neural Network.

29
00:01:22,460 --> 00:01:26,460
And then we'll go through an extension or a specific subset

30
00:01:26,460 --> 00:01:28,600
of RNNs called LSTMs.

31
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:31,460
That stands for Long Short-Term Memory.

32
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:33,620
And these actually seek to specifically avoid

33
00:01:33,620 --> 00:01:38,340
one of the common issues with RNNs, which is forgetting.

34
00:01:38,340 --> 00:01:41,180
And then we'll introduce the idea of sequence modeling

35
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:43,780
and long memory and start talking about some things

36
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:45,580
like autoregression.

37
00:01:45,580 --> 00:01:53,100
So if I look at this-- this is a single frame from a video--

38
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:55,660
there's a lot of things that computer vision can understand

39
00:01:55,660 --> 00:01:58,360
from this frame alone.

40
00:01:58,360 --> 00:02:02,200
So where for example-- and our human brains kind

41
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:03,500
do the same thing.

42
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:05,960
So if you look at just this frame,

43
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:08,860
can anyone tell me what this is a picture of?

44
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:14,245
Anything?

45
00:02:14,245 --> 00:02:15,338
AUDIENCE: Kids playing.

46
00:02:15,338 --> 00:02:16,380
SARA BEERY: Kids playing.

47
00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:18,000
Yeah.

48
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,020
And maybe they're playing in a classroom.

49
00:02:20,020 --> 00:02:22,020
So you can understand something about the scene.

50
00:02:22,020 --> 00:02:24,907
This looks like maybe a kindergarten classroom.

51
00:02:24,907 --> 00:02:27,240
We have computer vision models that can understand stuff

52
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:29,880
like objects that are seen in that single frame--

53
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:32,720
television, person, chair.

54
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:35,380
And we can start doing things like question answering,

55
00:02:35,380 --> 00:02:38,100
talking about specific attributes of those objects.

56
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:39,800
What color is the chair?

57
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:41,990
That chair is red.

58
00:02:41,990 --> 00:02:44,240
But so far, none of the things that we've talked about

59
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:47,620
have really explicitly tried to understand, for example,

60
00:02:47,620 --> 00:02:49,880
relationships between things and how we

61
00:02:49,880 --> 00:02:51,760
might predict into the future.

62
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:55,400
So for example, what will the girl do next?

63
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:58,390
That's something that from this given frame--

64
00:02:58,390 --> 00:03:01,270
I don't know-- probably we have some ideas

65
00:03:01,270 --> 00:03:04,830
about different possibilities, but we might not even know,

66
00:03:04,830 --> 00:03:06,970
because we also rely on temporal signal.

67
00:03:06,970 --> 00:03:10,230
We rely on context and information.

68
00:03:10,230 --> 00:03:12,590
But if we watch this video, and we

69
00:03:12,590 --> 00:03:15,230
get to the point of that frame, we

70
00:03:15,230 --> 00:03:18,570
can guess what's going to happen.

71
00:03:18,570 --> 00:03:21,550
[CHUCKLING]

72
00:03:21,550 --> 00:03:24,910
So I don't know if any of you have ever

73
00:03:24,910 --> 00:03:27,390
had this experience of a chair being pulled out

74
00:03:27,390 --> 00:03:30,750
from underneath you, and it's incredibly disorienting when

75
00:03:30,750 --> 00:03:34,150
something is not how you expect it might go.

76
00:03:34,150 --> 00:03:36,750
But watching this video, there hits a point

77
00:03:36,750 --> 00:03:38,910
in the video where we're pretty sure that the kind

78
00:03:38,910 --> 00:03:41,410
of likely thing, which is that the girl sits in the chair,

79
00:03:41,410 --> 00:03:43,850
is not going to happen because the guys moved the chair.

80
00:03:43,850 --> 00:03:45,550
There's these complex relationships

81
00:03:45,550 --> 00:03:48,110
between the actions.

82
00:03:48,110 --> 00:03:50,150
And then things like predicting she's

83
00:03:50,150 --> 00:03:54,070
going to fall, that she believes that the chair is there,

84
00:03:54,070 --> 00:03:57,410
why she believes that the chair is there, whether you guys are

85
00:03:57,410 --> 00:03:59,730
going to laugh at it.

86
00:03:59,730 --> 00:04:04,850
And so all of this is really very complex and nuanced

87
00:04:04,850 --> 00:04:07,970
understanding about the scenes, the objects, and things

88
00:04:07,970 --> 00:04:10,850
like intent, which we could argue

89
00:04:10,850 --> 00:04:13,010
about whether these machine learning models

90
00:04:13,010 --> 00:04:15,730
that model sequences are really getting at understanding

91
00:04:15,730 --> 00:04:17,290
things like human intent.

92
00:04:17,290 --> 00:04:21,410
But they do start to try to get at this sense of what

93
00:04:21,410 --> 00:04:23,890
is going to happen next.

94
00:04:23,890 --> 00:04:26,590
So sequences can be a lot of things.

95
00:04:26,590 --> 00:04:28,890
We talked previously when we introduced

96
00:04:28,890 --> 00:04:34,450
CNNs about the idea of adding a temporal dimension to things

97
00:04:34,450 --> 00:04:35,108
like images.

98
00:04:35,108 --> 00:04:36,150
So now, you have a video.

99
00:04:36,150 --> 00:04:41,830
This might be pictures from an Italian city square over time.

100
00:04:41,830 --> 00:04:44,530
You can also think about sequences of language.

101
00:04:44,530 --> 00:04:46,850
"An evening stroll through a city square"

102
00:04:46,850 --> 00:04:49,170
is a sequence of words.

103
00:04:49,170 --> 00:04:51,870
Or you could think about sequences of audio signal.

104
00:04:51,870 --> 00:04:55,230
Me saying "an evening stroll through a city square"

105
00:04:55,230 --> 00:04:57,950
is a sequence.

106
00:04:57,950 --> 00:05:00,368
And looking back again at the architecture

107
00:05:00,368 --> 00:05:01,910
that we've gotten very familiar with,

108
00:05:01,910 --> 00:05:04,390
convolutional neural networks, we

109
00:05:04,390 --> 00:05:07,750
can extend those into the space-time dimension

110
00:05:07,750 --> 00:05:10,170
and make like a space-time cube.

111
00:05:10,170 --> 00:05:12,990
And again, here, we're showing this in three dimensions

112
00:05:12,990 --> 00:05:14,930
to be for simplicity.

113
00:05:14,930 --> 00:05:17,312
But actually, it's really four dimensions.

114
00:05:17,312 --> 00:05:19,270
We're extending this fourth temporal dimension,

115
00:05:19,270 --> 00:05:22,670
because we have multiple inputs of red, green, and blue for each

116
00:05:22,670 --> 00:05:24,623
of those dimensions.

117
00:05:24,623 --> 00:05:26,790
And then time just becomes another spatial dimension

118
00:05:26,790 --> 00:05:27,990
in that tensor.

119
00:05:27,990 --> 00:05:31,150
So now, you have a 4D tensor instead of a 2D tensor.

120
00:05:31,150 --> 00:05:34,630
And so now, when we're thinking about how we might actually

121
00:05:34,630 --> 00:05:37,310
try to represent time, there's different ways

122
00:05:37,310 --> 00:05:40,890
that we can think about capturing time within this cube.

123
00:05:40,890 --> 00:05:44,750
And one interesting way that you can model temporal structure

124
00:05:44,750 --> 00:05:47,030
can be something like taking a slice

125
00:05:47,030 --> 00:05:48,870
through that space-time cube.

126
00:05:48,870 --> 00:05:53,370
So this is what it looks like if you build this new image that

127
00:05:53,370 --> 00:05:56,770
takes a single horizontal pixel slice

128
00:05:56,770 --> 00:05:59,290
and then runs that through time.

129
00:05:59,290 --> 00:06:00,990
So what are we looking at here?

130
00:06:00,990 --> 00:06:03,810
What are these stripes likely to be?

131
00:06:03,810 --> 00:06:05,370
AUDIENCE: People walking.

132
00:06:05,370 --> 00:06:08,170
SARA BEERY: Yeah, people walking, and walking

133
00:06:08,170 --> 00:06:09,930
at different angles to the camera

134
00:06:09,930 --> 00:06:13,450
or with different trajectories or different speeds.

135
00:06:13,450 --> 00:06:16,368
Or you could also think about taking a vertical slice

136
00:06:16,368 --> 00:06:16,910
through time.

137
00:06:16,910 --> 00:06:22,450
So now, this is showing, for a given position horizontally

138
00:06:22,450 --> 00:06:25,370
or vertically in the frame, who is going through that position

139
00:06:25,370 --> 00:06:26,070
first.

140
00:06:26,070 --> 00:06:28,650
Interestingly, even from a very long time ago,

141
00:06:28,650 --> 00:06:32,330
they started modeling images like this for the Olympics

142
00:06:32,330 --> 00:06:35,530
when they were trying to figure out who was the first person

143
00:06:35,530 --> 00:06:36,750
to go across the finish line.

144
00:06:36,750 --> 00:06:38,250
If you take the finish line, and you

145
00:06:38,250 --> 00:06:40,870
make that a single pixel element,

146
00:06:40,870 --> 00:06:43,530
you can see exactly what pixel crosses first,

147
00:06:43,530 --> 00:06:47,388
and that represents the person who won the race.

148
00:06:47,388 --> 00:06:48,930
And so there's lots of different ways

149
00:06:48,930 --> 00:06:51,538
you can think about representing or modeling time.

150
00:06:51,538 --> 00:06:53,830
And then if you actually want to think about how you do

151
00:06:53,830 --> 00:06:59,230
convolutions in time, if we take a very simple 1D perspective

152
00:06:59,230 --> 00:07:02,030
of this-- so now, imagine we're just talking about this scalar

153
00:07:02,030 --> 00:07:03,368
perspective.

154
00:07:03,368 --> 00:07:04,910
Then, what you would do is you would,

155
00:07:04,910 --> 00:07:07,430
similar to before, learn some weight

156
00:07:07,430 --> 00:07:10,250
matrix over some given fixed time window.

157
00:07:10,250 --> 00:07:14,550
So in this case, three elements in that sequence.

158
00:07:14,550 --> 00:07:18,390
And then you can think of this as a sequence-to-sequence model.

159
00:07:18,390 --> 00:07:20,950
So instead of before, where we took an image or something,

160
00:07:20,950 --> 00:07:23,150
and we would run this across the image

161
00:07:23,150 --> 00:07:25,470
in these different patches, and then

162
00:07:25,470 --> 00:07:28,190
you were constrained by the structure of the image,

163
00:07:28,190 --> 00:07:31,450
here actually, now, you can run this in time.

164
00:07:31,450 --> 00:07:35,350
So now, you're taking input from these three different positions

165
00:07:35,350 --> 00:07:38,330
in the sequence, and you're generating an output position.

166
00:07:38,330 --> 00:07:42,430
Now, you could slide that, and you could slide it arbitrarily

167
00:07:42,430 --> 00:07:43,430
long.

168
00:07:43,430 --> 00:07:46,310
You can take any arbitrary length sequence

169
00:07:46,310 --> 00:07:49,230
and you can generate an arbitrary length sequence.

170
00:07:49,230 --> 00:07:52,160
So these convolutions in time, they're not constrained.

171
00:07:52,160 --> 00:07:54,660
I mean, they're only constrained by the length of your input

172
00:07:54,660 --> 00:07:55,200
sequence.

173
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:57,380
And you can imagine that if you had something

174
00:07:57,380 --> 00:08:00,380
like a static camera that was taking video of this classroom

175
00:08:00,380 --> 00:08:02,380
continuously, that you could just

176
00:08:02,380 --> 00:08:04,520
keep running these convolutions in time,

177
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:06,140
and there wouldn't necessarily be

178
00:08:06,140 --> 00:08:08,460
a point where you needed to stop or were constrained

179
00:08:08,460 --> 00:08:11,700
by the architecture itself.

180
00:08:11,700 --> 00:08:15,860
Now, if you consider something like more dimensions,

181
00:08:15,860 --> 00:08:20,860
things like these RGB videos or add even additional dimensions

182
00:08:20,860 --> 00:08:24,020
in that RGB channel, something like MRI that captures 3D

183
00:08:24,020 --> 00:08:27,260
volumes over time, or hyperspectral satellite images

184
00:08:27,260 --> 00:08:33,140
that can have something like 384 different bands,

185
00:08:33,140 --> 00:08:35,679
you can continue to think about these convolutions,

186
00:08:35,679 --> 00:08:38,820
but now where time is potentially this infinitely long

187
00:08:38,820 --> 00:08:41,419
dimension.

188
00:08:41,419 --> 00:08:47,380
And if you're doing this, then what we discover both

189
00:08:47,380 --> 00:08:50,400
empirically-- and also it kind of makes sense intuitively--

190
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:55,560
is that as that time sequence becomes arbitrarily long,

191
00:08:55,560 --> 00:08:57,360
it's hard for our memories of what

192
00:08:57,360 --> 00:09:00,600
happened at the beginning of the sequence to persist.

193
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:05,040
And one of the reasons for this with a convolutional approach

194
00:09:05,040 --> 00:09:07,300
is the following.

195
00:09:07,300 --> 00:09:12,640
So here, say we have our temporal convolution,

196
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:14,420
and it's taking in some input.

197
00:09:14,420 --> 00:09:16,540
We're showing the scalar version,

198
00:09:16,540 --> 00:09:20,200
but for the sake of intuition, this

199
00:09:20,200 --> 00:09:23,600
is a picture of my cat Frank, who

200
00:09:23,600 --> 00:09:26,120
is the cutest cat in the world.

201
00:09:26,120 --> 00:09:30,560
And so say I'm walking around my house taking a video.

202
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:32,740
And I start at this point in time.

203
00:09:32,740 --> 00:09:34,400
And then I go forward.

204
00:09:34,400 --> 00:09:37,592
And now, in the future, I have again--

205
00:09:37,592 --> 00:09:39,800
remember, because it's convolution-- that same weight

206
00:09:39,800 --> 00:09:40,800
matrix.

207
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:43,920
And now, I want to understand what's in here.

208
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:46,120
But the thing is I don't really have,

209
00:09:46,120 --> 00:09:49,420
from this fixed time window, any inputs

210
00:09:49,420 --> 00:09:50,760
coming from the beginning.

211
00:09:50,760 --> 00:09:53,520
I've only got the window that I can look at.

212
00:09:53,520 --> 00:09:55,980
And so, based on the information I have--

213
00:09:55,980 --> 00:09:59,180
well, maybe I look at this and I predict

214
00:09:59,180 --> 00:10:02,380
tiger, because it's orange and it's outside,

215
00:10:02,380 --> 00:10:05,980
and cats are usually indoors.

216
00:10:05,980 --> 00:10:10,180
So the idea of something like memory

217
00:10:10,180 --> 00:10:14,900
or trying to extend what we forget, move beyond these fixed

218
00:10:14,900 --> 00:10:15,980
time windows--

219
00:10:15,980 --> 00:10:18,880
is to try to capture that context,

220
00:10:18,880 --> 00:10:20,800
be able to remember what you saw in the past.

221
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:23,300
So here then, maybe you'd have some memory unit,

222
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:25,240
and that would actually give you some context,

223
00:10:25,240 --> 00:10:26,860
give you some information.

224
00:10:26,860 --> 00:10:28,655
Since I'm walking around my house,

225
00:10:28,655 --> 00:10:30,280
it's unlikely I'm going to see a tiger.

226
00:10:30,280 --> 00:10:34,940
But I did see Frank before, so it's maybe still Frank.

227
00:10:34,940 --> 00:10:38,460
And so this type of identification

228
00:10:38,460 --> 00:10:39,980
with the context of what you've seen

229
00:10:39,980 --> 00:10:43,900
in the past from the same, for example, sequence

230
00:10:43,900 --> 00:10:46,580
is really the intuition or the motivation

231
00:10:46,580 --> 00:10:48,280
behind the development of what we

232
00:10:48,280 --> 00:10:51,520
call recurrent neural networks, so these RNNs.

233
00:10:51,520 --> 00:10:55,280
So transformers, CNNs, everything so far

234
00:10:55,280 --> 00:10:58,180
mostly had of fixed window in time.

235
00:10:58,180 --> 00:11:01,320
There was transformers these days--

236
00:11:01,320 --> 00:11:04,320
we can talk about that context window is getting very, very

237
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:07,320
large, but it's still fixed.

238
00:11:07,320 --> 00:11:11,383
But a recurrent neural network makes that infinite.

239
00:11:11,383 --> 00:11:12,800
And the way that it does it is you

240
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:16,800
introduce the idea of a hidden unit.

241
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:20,560
So now, you have-- for example, from this convolution

242
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:23,060
you send-- you run your model through the convolution.

243
00:11:23,060 --> 00:11:25,000
You get some value.

244
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:30,360
And then you learn a way to take that value, that hidden unit,

245
00:11:30,360 --> 00:11:32,280
and predict the output.

246
00:11:32,280 --> 00:11:34,240
But you also take that hidden unit,

247
00:11:34,240 --> 00:11:37,440
and you send it forward in time to the next time step.

248
00:11:37,440 --> 00:11:41,040
So here, now, as you're convolving through time,

249
00:11:41,040 --> 00:11:44,360
you're actually taking in both that new temporal input

250
00:11:44,360 --> 00:11:46,730
and your memory from the past.

251
00:11:49,590 --> 00:11:52,070
So here, if we want to look at this in a little bit

252
00:11:52,070 --> 00:11:54,870
more detail, again, just thinking of everything as

253
00:11:54,870 --> 00:11:58,990
scalars just because it's really easy to visualize this way.

254
00:11:58,990 --> 00:12:02,137
So a version of a scalar temporal prediction problem

255
00:12:02,137 --> 00:12:04,470
might be something like predicting temperature in Boston

256
00:12:04,470 --> 00:12:06,350
tomorrow.

257
00:12:06,350 --> 00:12:09,950
So here, now, what you're doing is

258
00:12:09,950 --> 00:12:13,250
you're representing time as some of discrete sequence,

259
00:12:13,250 --> 00:12:16,870
though you can apply these to continual sequences.

260
00:12:16,870 --> 00:12:19,110
And the next hidden state-- so now,

261
00:12:19,110 --> 00:12:21,670
we have our inputs running through some model

262
00:12:21,670 --> 00:12:23,150
to get some hidden state.

263
00:12:23,150 --> 00:12:25,070
And then we send this output, and we also

264
00:12:25,070 --> 00:12:27,910
send that hidden state one time step forward.

265
00:12:27,910 --> 00:12:31,390
So here, it's as you're kind of running this through,

266
00:12:31,390 --> 00:12:36,470
what we do is we say your next hidden state is represented

267
00:12:36,470 --> 00:12:38,910
by some function f that will depend

268
00:12:38,910 --> 00:12:42,550
on the previous hidden state and the current input.

269
00:12:42,550 --> 00:12:46,090
And then that f, that function, that's shared over time.

270
00:12:46,090 --> 00:12:50,430
So that function is, again, fixed over time.

271
00:12:50,430 --> 00:12:54,030
The way that we push information forward is fixed over time,

272
00:12:54,030 --> 00:12:56,530
and that's something that gets learned.

273
00:12:56,530 --> 00:12:59,610
And it's independent of time what that function is.

274
00:12:59,610 --> 00:13:04,250
And then similarly, we'll have learned some function g that

275
00:13:04,250 --> 00:13:07,450
will map from our hidden state to our output prediction

276
00:13:07,450 --> 00:13:09,210
for any given time step.

277
00:13:09,210 --> 00:13:12,010
And that's also going to be shared over time.

278
00:13:12,010 --> 00:13:18,290
And so here, you'll note that this is the first time we're not

279
00:13:18,290 --> 00:13:19,670
seeing a DAG.

280
00:13:19,670 --> 00:13:22,310
This is not a Directed Acyclic Graph.

281
00:13:22,310 --> 00:13:22,990
We have a loop.

282
00:13:22,990 --> 00:13:24,090
We have a cycle.

283
00:13:24,090 --> 00:13:27,090
Because you're going from that hidden state to the hidden state

284
00:13:27,090 --> 00:13:29,010
again.

285
00:13:29,010 --> 00:13:30,030
And that's recurrent.

286
00:13:30,030 --> 00:13:33,730
And so you can write this recurrent function

287
00:13:33,730 --> 00:13:35,610
for what your hidden state will be,

288
00:13:35,610 --> 00:13:37,770
that some function of your previous hidden state

289
00:13:37,770 --> 00:13:40,370
and your current input beta.

290
00:13:40,370 --> 00:13:42,570
And then again your output function

291
00:13:42,570 --> 00:13:47,750
is going to be g mapped from your hidden state.

292
00:13:47,750 --> 00:13:50,430
I think it's a little easier to interpret

293
00:13:50,430 --> 00:13:52,670
when you think about the computation being unrolled,

294
00:13:52,670 --> 00:13:56,190
because then, actually, for any given time point,

295
00:13:56,190 --> 00:13:58,330
it's still a directed graph.

296
00:13:58,330 --> 00:14:01,590
It's just that you have this recurrence loop.

297
00:14:01,590 --> 00:14:05,962
So again, this is the simplest version of this.

298
00:14:05,962 --> 00:14:07,670
So now, you assume all of those functions

299
00:14:07,670 --> 00:14:09,310
are linear layers, which we're very

300
00:14:09,310 --> 00:14:10,950
familiar with at this point.

301
00:14:10,950 --> 00:14:12,670
So now, what you're learning is you're

302
00:14:12,670 --> 00:14:19,008
learning u, which is mapping your input to the next point.

303
00:14:19,008 --> 00:14:20,550
You're learning some v that's mapping

304
00:14:20,550 --> 00:14:24,700
your previous hidden state in as well, and you combine them.

305
00:14:24,700 --> 00:14:26,950
And then you're learning v that maps your hidden state

306
00:14:26,950 --> 00:14:29,710
to the output.

307
00:14:29,710 --> 00:14:33,210
And so now, here, you have something like this right.

308
00:14:33,210 --> 00:14:37,070
You'll have some non-linearities in there in the simplest form.

309
00:14:37,070 --> 00:14:40,670
So your new hidden state is just non-linearity applied

310
00:14:40,670 --> 00:14:44,130
on top of w times the previous hidden state,

311
00:14:44,130 --> 00:14:46,770
plus u times your input data at the current time

312
00:14:46,770 --> 00:14:49,290
point, plus some bias term.

313
00:14:49,290 --> 00:14:51,410
And then similarly, the output again

314
00:14:51,410 --> 00:14:56,570
is some other nonlinearity times V

315
00:14:56,570 --> 00:14:59,850
plus the current hidden state plus a different bias term.

316
00:14:59,850 --> 00:15:07,170
So if I got rid of W here, what would this be called--

317
00:15:07,170 --> 00:15:09,670
if I got rid of the kind of recurrence dimension,

318
00:15:09,670 --> 00:15:13,090
if we were just looking at that mapping between the input

319
00:15:13,090 --> 00:15:13,950
and the output?

320
00:15:19,370 --> 00:15:22,350
I mean, since it's scalars, it's a little silly.

321
00:15:22,350 --> 00:15:24,510
I mean, it's like a one-dimensional convolution.

322
00:15:24,510 --> 00:15:26,470
But imagine if you extend these beyond scalars.

323
00:15:26,470 --> 00:15:28,890
It starts looking a little bit like that multi-layer perceptron

324
00:15:28,890 --> 00:15:30,230
that we're very familiar with.

325
00:15:30,230 --> 00:15:33,890
So the only difference here is that recurrence

326
00:15:33,890 --> 00:15:37,530
that's actually sending this information forward over time.

327
00:15:37,530 --> 00:15:39,190
And really, I think, the point here

328
00:15:39,190 --> 00:15:40,690
is that the hidden state is intended

329
00:15:40,690 --> 00:15:45,950
to capture the most relevant historical information.

330
00:15:45,950 --> 00:15:50,550
And what we're learning in W is how

331
00:15:50,550 --> 00:15:53,350
do we actually understand what is relevant?

332
00:15:53,350 --> 00:15:56,070
What do we need to keep?

333
00:15:56,070 --> 00:15:58,670
And of course, this can be extended to be deep.

334
00:15:58,670 --> 00:16:01,090
You don't need to only have a single hidden state.

335
00:16:01,090 --> 00:16:03,930
You can actually stack a bunch of these on top of each other--

336
00:16:03,930 --> 00:16:08,550
have many, many different hidden states, hidden layers.

337
00:16:08,550 --> 00:16:12,210
And then, now, these could be any dimensional tensor.

338
00:16:12,210 --> 00:16:14,653
And there are many, many, many works

339
00:16:14,653 --> 00:16:16,070
that have looked at different ways

340
00:16:16,070 --> 00:16:19,910
to structure these deep recurrent neural networks.

341
00:16:19,910 --> 00:16:22,310
But how do we do backprop if we don't have a DAG?

342
00:16:22,310 --> 00:16:24,616
Oh, yeah?

343
00:16:24,616 --> 00:16:28,950
AUDIENCE: So the recurrence step-- how many time

344
00:16:28,950 --> 00:16:29,830
steps do you have?

345
00:16:29,830 --> 00:16:32,670
So you're unrolling it, but how do you know when to stop?

346
00:16:32,670 --> 00:16:34,350
SARA BEERY: To infinity and beyond.

347
00:16:34,350 --> 00:16:35,510
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

348
00:16:35,510 --> 00:16:37,130
Is it a hyperparameter?

349
00:16:37,130 --> 00:16:39,580
Is it something that gets learned in the network

350
00:16:39,580 --> 00:16:43,010
to see what's the optimal number of these--

351
00:16:43,010 --> 00:16:44,760
SARA BEERY: So the way this is structured,

352
00:16:44,760 --> 00:16:46,900
it doesn't have a sense of stop.

353
00:16:46,900 --> 00:16:49,680
I mean, it'll just stop when you stop doing it.

354
00:16:49,680 --> 00:16:51,432
Like, when you stop putting in input data,

355
00:16:51,432 --> 00:16:52,640
I guess that's when it stops.

356
00:16:52,640 --> 00:16:54,420
But there isn't a sense in this model

357
00:16:54,420 --> 00:16:57,580
necessarily, as it's structured, that has an end.

358
00:16:57,580 --> 00:16:59,940
Because you have that weight that's

359
00:16:59,940 --> 00:17:03,360
passing the previous hidden unit into the next one.

360
00:17:03,360 --> 00:17:06,319
So if you don't give it a next step, I guess that's the end.

361
00:17:06,319 --> 00:17:09,140
But it doesn't know what's stopping it.

362
00:17:09,140 --> 00:17:12,619
AUDIENCE: In practice, are you-- is your input like a sequence,

363
00:17:12,619 --> 00:17:16,460
or are you feeding it in one-by-one?

364
00:17:16,460 --> 00:17:19,300
SARA BEERY: I mean, it depends on the problem.

365
00:17:19,300 --> 00:17:22,980
I mean, if you're using these types of RNNs

366
00:17:22,980 --> 00:17:27,200
to do something like model video or model audio clips,

367
00:17:27,200 --> 00:17:28,540
they probably have an end.

368
00:17:28,540 --> 00:17:31,260
So you just stop running them when you get to the end.

369
00:17:31,260 --> 00:17:34,640
But you could also think of it in a continual way,

370
00:17:34,640 --> 00:17:37,582
like maybe you're continuing to get temperature data every day,

371
00:17:37,582 --> 00:17:39,540
and every day you predict the next temperature,

372
00:17:39,540 --> 00:17:42,180
and that's going to go until the world ends.

373
00:17:45,145 --> 00:17:46,020
Does that make sense?

374
00:17:46,020 --> 00:17:46,540
Yeah?

375
00:17:46,540 --> 00:17:48,040
AUDIENCE: How does this architecture

376
00:17:48,040 --> 00:17:50,240
capture the infinite life of the memory?

377
00:17:50,240 --> 00:17:53,520
Because in a way, it seems like a Markov chain.

378
00:17:53,520 --> 00:17:56,240
It only depends on the previous state.

379
00:17:56,240 --> 00:17:59,040
SARA BEERY: So the question was, how does this architecture

380
00:17:59,040 --> 00:18:02,420
capture memory throughout that very long infinite length,

381
00:18:02,420 --> 00:18:05,240
because that hidden state only depends on the previous state?

382
00:18:05,240 --> 00:18:06,680
So it's recurrent.

383
00:18:06,680 --> 00:18:09,085
So this state only depends on the previous state.

384
00:18:09,085 --> 00:18:11,460
But the previous state depended on the state before that.

385
00:18:11,460 --> 00:18:13,835
And the previous state depended on the state before that.

386
00:18:13,835 --> 00:18:17,320
So you are capturing in every single hidden unit

387
00:18:17,320 --> 00:18:19,240
as far back as you have.

388
00:18:19,240 --> 00:18:22,360
But we'll talk about how even though that's theoretically

389
00:18:22,360 --> 00:18:24,640
true, that this construction actually

390
00:18:24,640 --> 00:18:28,360
makes it really difficult to learn things across very, very

391
00:18:28,360 --> 00:18:33,840
long time horizons, mostly because you have some limited

392
00:18:33,840 --> 00:18:34,647
capacity there.

393
00:18:34,647 --> 00:18:36,480
And then we'll also talk about actually some

394
00:18:36,480 --> 00:18:39,320
of the mathematical limitations of this formulation.

395
00:18:39,320 --> 00:18:42,000
But yes, great question.

396
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:42,500
Cool.

397
00:18:42,500 --> 00:18:44,460
So how do you do backprop if we have

398
00:18:44,460 --> 00:18:47,340
a DAG-- if we don't have a DAG?

399
00:18:47,340 --> 00:18:49,860
So the simple, practical thing-- historically,

400
00:18:49,860 --> 00:18:52,260
this was something that really tripped people up.

401
00:18:52,260 --> 00:18:54,620
Backprop is intended for feedforward networks,

402
00:18:54,620 --> 00:18:57,020
and now we have loops.

403
00:18:57,020 --> 00:19:00,260
But the hack is you just decide when

404
00:19:00,260 --> 00:19:03,860
you're training what your fixed time window that you're going

405
00:19:03,860 --> 00:19:06,580
to propagate back through is.

406
00:19:06,580 --> 00:19:11,220
And now, you unroll it through that time window.

407
00:19:11,220 --> 00:19:13,775
So you'd say, guess what?

408
00:19:13,775 --> 00:19:15,400
My time window is going to be this far.

409
00:19:15,400 --> 00:19:18,700
I'm going to go from V back to x0.

410
00:19:18,700 --> 00:19:20,823
And then, now, you've unrolled it.

411
00:19:20,823 --> 00:19:22,740
And of course, you have some shared parameters

412
00:19:22,740 --> 00:19:24,260
in that network, but it's actually

413
00:19:24,260 --> 00:19:28,220
now, again, a directed acyclic graph, because we're

414
00:19:28,220 --> 00:19:29,780
stopping the point where you're going

415
00:19:29,780 --> 00:19:31,980
to keep going back forever.

416
00:19:31,980 --> 00:19:34,620
And so here, you can actually just-- again,

417
00:19:34,620 --> 00:19:36,480
if you're going to try to calculate

418
00:19:36,480 --> 00:19:44,760
the influence on that output, d of xout with respect to d of xin

419
00:19:44,760 --> 00:19:48,600
over from 0 to time t, you can do that

420
00:19:48,600 --> 00:19:54,080
by just sending now through this directed acyclic graph back

421
00:19:54,080 --> 00:19:57,880
to that zeroth element just by unrolling

422
00:19:57,880 --> 00:20:00,480
as far as you want to unroll.

423
00:20:00,480 --> 00:20:03,660
And that's a parameter that you would select or tune.

424
00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:08,520
How far are you going to send your backpropagation?

425
00:20:08,520 --> 00:20:10,440
And then once you've unrolled it,

426
00:20:10,440 --> 00:20:12,320
and you've kind of decided on this fixed time

427
00:20:12,320 --> 00:20:14,280
window during training, then you can just

428
00:20:14,280 --> 00:20:16,240
use the same chain rule.

429
00:20:16,240 --> 00:20:19,760
And you can calculate what that gradient is going to be.

430
00:20:19,760 --> 00:20:21,200
And the interesting thing here is

431
00:20:21,200 --> 00:20:24,800
it does limit maybe how well we might learn things

432
00:20:24,800 --> 00:20:27,960
further back in time because, of course,

433
00:20:27,960 --> 00:20:30,618
the information is still available.

434
00:20:30,618 --> 00:20:32,160
At inference time, you're still going

435
00:20:32,160 --> 00:20:34,160
to have that previous information getting passed

436
00:20:34,160 --> 00:20:35,600
through or getting encoded.

437
00:20:35,600 --> 00:20:38,340
But if you only trained the model to send signal back

438
00:20:38,340 --> 00:20:41,100
to a given fixed time window, you

439
00:20:41,100 --> 00:20:43,060
could see how intuitively it might

440
00:20:43,060 --> 00:20:48,340
teach you to only capture some of limited

441
00:20:48,340 --> 00:20:50,580
temporal window of information.

442
00:20:50,580 --> 00:20:56,300
Now, in practice, there's also other complexities

443
00:20:56,300 --> 00:20:59,760
as we increase that time window in terms of what that means.

444
00:20:59,760 --> 00:21:00,260
Yeah?

445
00:21:00,260 --> 00:21:03,340
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] increase the time window, is it then

446
00:21:03,340 --> 00:21:07,220
harder to predict if you only use a couple of inputs

447
00:21:07,220 --> 00:21:09,580
at inference time?

448
00:21:09,580 --> 00:21:10,772
SARA BEERY: Oh, interesting.

449
00:21:10,772 --> 00:21:12,980
So the question, which I think is an interesting one,

450
00:21:12,980 --> 00:21:18,740
is if you've trained this model using backprop over maybe a time

451
00:21:18,740 --> 00:21:25,180
window of 20, now if you only have a sequence of three inputs,

452
00:21:25,180 --> 00:21:28,240
is that actually limiting your ability to do it shorter?

453
00:21:28,240 --> 00:21:32,820
So potentially no, because this is just

454
00:21:32,820 --> 00:21:38,610
looking at one component of that gradient.

455
00:21:38,610 --> 00:21:43,770
But actually, if we think about this, you--

456
00:21:43,770 --> 00:21:47,130
so this gradient is also going to be--

457
00:21:47,130 --> 00:21:50,990
this is just the input of that on that,

458
00:21:50,990 --> 00:21:53,170
and that's like the end of that time limit.

459
00:21:53,170 --> 00:21:56,730
But in practice, you would also do everything up

460
00:21:56,730 --> 00:21:58,030
to this time point.

461
00:21:58,030 --> 00:22:00,210
And we'll go through that in the next couple slides.

462
00:22:00,210 --> 00:22:02,953
So you would still be getting that signal from everything

463
00:22:02,953 --> 00:22:04,870
within the time window that you're looking at,

464
00:22:04,870 --> 00:22:07,510
not just going back to the farthest point.

465
00:22:07,510 --> 00:22:11,410
Which makes sense, because it might be much more

466
00:22:11,410 --> 00:22:14,490
helpful to have some of those more frequent, more

467
00:22:14,490 --> 00:22:17,190
recent gradients than maybe the further ones.

468
00:22:17,190 --> 00:22:18,290
Yeah?

469
00:22:18,290 --> 00:22:20,790
AUDIENCE: To go along that point, for this example,

470
00:22:20,790 --> 00:22:24,250
would it be like if xin is today's temperature,

471
00:22:24,250 --> 00:22:27,550
xout is six days in advance temperature.

472
00:22:27,550 --> 00:22:30,570
But we are actually inherently predicting

473
00:22:30,570 --> 00:22:34,597
one day, two day, three day, four day, five day to get there.

474
00:22:34,597 --> 00:22:36,430
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so what we're showing here

475
00:22:36,430 --> 00:22:38,890
is just the fact that if you do unroll this,

476
00:22:38,890 --> 00:22:40,973
that now you do have this directed graph again.

477
00:22:40,973 --> 00:22:43,390
But then like in practice, essentially, what you're saying

478
00:22:43,390 --> 00:22:45,430
is you would only take the gradients up

479
00:22:45,430 --> 00:22:48,050
to t minus whatever x0 was.

480
00:22:48,050 --> 00:22:50,485
So this is like 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.

481
00:22:50,485 --> 00:22:52,110
So you'd only be taking those gradients

482
00:22:52,110 --> 00:22:54,230
over a sequence of six.

483
00:22:54,230 --> 00:22:57,810
And then, now, for the next point you'd shift it right.

484
00:22:57,810 --> 00:22:59,330
So now, you'd only go to x1.

485
00:22:59,330 --> 00:23:00,747
And then for the next point, you'd

486
00:23:00,747 --> 00:23:02,350
only send gradients from x2.

487
00:23:02,350 --> 00:23:03,830
So you have some fixed time window

488
00:23:03,830 --> 00:23:06,410
that you're unrolling that you actually accept gradient--

489
00:23:06,410 --> 00:23:09,170
you calculate gradients over.

490
00:23:09,170 --> 00:23:09,670
Yeah?

491
00:23:09,670 --> 00:23:11,630
AUDIENCE: The idea that the truncated time

492
00:23:11,630 --> 00:23:14,645
window is some approximation of an infinite time window?

493
00:23:14,645 --> 00:23:16,770
SARA BEERY: I think that's a reasonable assumption.

494
00:23:16,770 --> 00:23:18,630
So the question was is that fixed time

495
00:23:18,630 --> 00:23:20,070
window a reasonable approximation

496
00:23:20,070 --> 00:23:21,710
of an infinite time window?

497
00:23:21,710 --> 00:23:23,830
I would say it's maybe a bit of both.

498
00:23:23,830 --> 00:23:26,330
So again, I'll walk through it in a sec,

499
00:23:26,330 --> 00:23:29,590
but basically, with this formulation, the longer time

500
00:23:29,590 --> 00:23:32,250
window you have, the harder it is for information

501
00:23:32,250 --> 00:23:35,930
to actually make it that far with these weights.

502
00:23:35,930 --> 00:23:38,730
So at some point, actually, extending the time window

503
00:23:38,730 --> 00:23:42,550
is probably, by construction here, not super beneficial.

504
00:23:46,130 --> 00:23:46,630
Yes?

505
00:23:46,630 --> 00:23:48,047
AUDIENCE: I wonder, is there a way

506
00:23:48,047 --> 00:23:49,810
to optimize what's the time window you

507
00:23:49,810 --> 00:23:51,070
need for a specific task?

508
00:23:51,070 --> 00:23:52,903
Or is it just--

509
00:23:52,903 --> 00:23:54,570
SARA BEERY: Well, it's a hyperparameter.

510
00:23:54,570 --> 00:23:57,090
So when you look at the literature for this,

511
00:23:57,090 --> 00:24:00,930
people would ablate that hyperparameter, for example.

512
00:24:00,930 --> 00:24:04,228
And I think sometimes, it might be something

513
00:24:04,228 --> 00:24:06,770
that you could actually bring some domain knowledge to decide

514
00:24:06,770 --> 00:24:09,030
what actually makes sense here.

515
00:24:11,890 --> 00:24:14,270
So a few conceptual things.

516
00:24:14,270 --> 00:24:20,170
If you are going to unroll this, remember that function

517
00:24:20,170 --> 00:24:22,570
that maps the hidden states forward through time

518
00:24:22,570 --> 00:24:25,250
is fixed over time.

519
00:24:25,250 --> 00:24:28,690
So those W's are shared.

520
00:24:28,690 --> 00:24:31,690
So now, what does that mean for backprop?

521
00:24:31,690 --> 00:24:35,070
So we have this sequence of videos.

522
00:24:35,070 --> 00:24:39,510
Now, the loss is going to end up being

523
00:24:39,510 --> 00:24:42,710
the sum of the losses from each of those predicted

524
00:24:42,710 --> 00:24:45,990
frames over some sequence.

525
00:24:45,990 --> 00:24:48,070
And to optimize, you want to learn

526
00:24:48,070 --> 00:24:52,230
how you would change W, U, and V so

527
00:24:52,230 --> 00:25:00,310
that the sum of the kind of loss over that sequence is minimized.

528
00:25:00,310 --> 00:25:01,930
This is how we do gradient descent.

529
00:25:01,930 --> 00:25:04,530
But you want it for the entire sequence within, I guess,

530
00:25:04,530 --> 00:25:07,110
the time window that you're considering.

531
00:25:07,110 --> 00:25:09,890
So we want to figure out how we want to change all of these.

532
00:25:09,890 --> 00:25:14,270
But of course, W is, again, shared over time.

533
00:25:14,270 --> 00:25:18,070
So we have to do backprop over shared parameters.

534
00:25:18,070 --> 00:25:21,670
So we're going to sum up all of these individual losses.

535
00:25:21,670 --> 00:25:24,510
And now, we're going to be passing this gradient back.

536
00:25:24,510 --> 00:25:27,870
And now, does anyone remember what

537
00:25:27,870 --> 00:25:31,260
it looks like when we have these backprop over shared parameters?

538
00:25:31,260 --> 00:25:34,340
Because here, we have the loss is all

539
00:25:34,340 --> 00:25:36,780
getting summed up, the gradients all coming down.

540
00:25:36,780 --> 00:25:39,580
And then there's this kind of waterfall backwards where those

541
00:25:39,580 --> 00:25:42,100
gradients are passing through all the W's.

542
00:25:42,100 --> 00:25:44,940
So does anyone remember what we do when we're taking gradients

543
00:25:44,940 --> 00:25:46,400
over shared parameters?

544
00:25:50,020 --> 00:25:51,540
It's been a couple of weeks.

545
00:25:51,540 --> 00:25:53,820
Does this look familiar to anybody?

546
00:25:53,820 --> 00:25:57,340
Yeah, so here, there's this idea that if you

547
00:25:57,340 --> 00:25:59,020
had shared parameters, and they're

548
00:25:59,020 --> 00:26:01,340
being shared throughout a network,

549
00:26:01,340 --> 00:26:04,500
that it's actually the same as branching structure

550
00:26:04,500 --> 00:26:06,660
in a directed acyclic graph.

551
00:26:06,660 --> 00:26:09,620
And so when you're doing a gradient-- when you're

552
00:26:09,620 --> 00:26:12,040
taking the gradient over this branching structure,

553
00:26:12,040 --> 00:26:15,500
you're actually just summing the gradients for the parameter

554
00:26:15,500 --> 00:26:17,700
each time that parameter occurs.

555
00:26:17,700 --> 00:26:19,340
So for that W, we're going to just

556
00:26:19,340 --> 00:26:22,420
be summing those gradients over every single element

557
00:26:22,420 --> 00:26:25,300
that we have that W in.

558
00:26:25,300 --> 00:26:25,940
Cool.

559
00:26:25,940 --> 00:26:29,260
So here, we have W in all of these places,

560
00:26:29,260 --> 00:26:31,280
and now we have this branching structure.

561
00:26:31,280 --> 00:26:33,520
And then when we're calculating the change

562
00:26:33,520 --> 00:26:36,120
in that loss with respect to W, it's

563
00:26:36,120 --> 00:26:39,920
the sum over the change in the loss, the gradients from each

564
00:26:39,920 --> 00:26:41,540
of those.

565
00:26:41,540 --> 00:26:42,040
Cool.

566
00:26:42,040 --> 00:26:45,360
And the nice thing is, if you're using something like PyTorch,

567
00:26:45,360 --> 00:26:47,320
this will happen automatically in autograd

568
00:26:47,320 --> 00:26:50,200
as long as you don't do deep copies, as long as you're

569
00:26:50,200 --> 00:26:52,860
pointing to the actual by reference

570
00:26:52,860 --> 00:26:54,800
that actual variable, then autograd

571
00:26:54,800 --> 00:26:58,040
handles this kind of summing over

572
00:26:58,040 --> 00:27:00,640
the gradients within the thing reasonably well.

573
00:27:00,640 --> 00:27:02,720
So make sure you're not doing deep copies,

574
00:27:02,720 --> 00:27:06,200
and it all works out pretty nicely.

575
00:27:06,200 --> 00:27:09,200
And then I think another point is you're

576
00:27:09,200 --> 00:27:10,940
summing over the past inputs.

577
00:27:10,940 --> 00:27:13,645
So if you're calculating the loss with respect to this one,

578
00:27:13,645 --> 00:27:15,020
you're summing over all of those.

579
00:27:15,020 --> 00:27:16,978
If you're calculating with respect to this one,

580
00:27:16,978 --> 00:27:19,840
it's only over the ones previous to it, et cetera, et cetera,

581
00:27:19,840 --> 00:27:23,120
because that's only going in one direction.

582
00:27:23,120 --> 00:27:29,380
Any other questions about backpropagation through an RNN?

583
00:27:29,380 --> 00:27:31,780
Cool, let's move on.

584
00:27:31,780 --> 00:27:37,240
So problem of long-range dependencies.

585
00:27:37,240 --> 00:27:40,220
We talked about, well, as this time window starts

586
00:27:40,220 --> 00:27:42,100
to stretch towards infinity, it seems

587
00:27:42,100 --> 00:27:45,060
like it would be hard for this thing to remember.

588
00:27:45,060 --> 00:27:48,300
Why not set your time window to infinity?

589
00:27:48,300 --> 00:27:50,340
Why not remember everything?

590
00:27:50,340 --> 00:27:55,720
So it turns out, first, memory size will grow with that t.

591
00:27:55,720 --> 00:27:58,140
So if you need to use some of infinitely long sequence

592
00:27:58,140 --> 00:28:01,500
as an input, that means it takes up a lot more memory.

593
00:28:01,500 --> 00:28:05,020
And that time and memory is non-parametric.

594
00:28:05,020 --> 00:28:06,900
There's not like a finite set of parameters

595
00:28:06,900 --> 00:28:10,660
we can use to model infinite time.

596
00:28:10,660 --> 00:28:13,940
RNNs make like a Markov assumption,

597
00:28:13,940 --> 00:28:17,340
which is to say that the future hidden state only

598
00:28:17,340 --> 00:28:20,580
depends on the immediate preceding hidden state.

599
00:28:20,580 --> 00:28:23,700
So we say, it's recurrent, but we only

600
00:28:23,700 --> 00:28:26,960
depend on just the immediate hidden state before that.

601
00:28:26,960 --> 00:28:29,277
And then by putting the right information

602
00:28:29,277 --> 00:28:31,360
into that hidden state, you can model dependencies

603
00:28:31,360 --> 00:28:33,560
that are arbitrarily far apart in theory,

604
00:28:33,560 --> 00:28:37,120
because the model learns with W how

605
00:28:37,120 --> 00:28:39,000
to keep the relevant information,

606
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:42,080
or at least that's the hope.

607
00:28:42,080 --> 00:28:46,600
So here, what you're saying is, actually, in order

608
00:28:46,600 --> 00:28:48,920
to capture those long range dependencies,

609
00:28:48,920 --> 00:28:51,400
we have to propagate that information

610
00:28:51,400 --> 00:28:55,800
through a very long chain of dependencies.

611
00:28:55,800 --> 00:29:00,680
And this ends up being a bit difficult.

612
00:29:00,680 --> 00:29:08,480
So let's walk through this-- this very big chalk.

613
00:29:08,480 --> 00:29:11,680
So for simplicity, just to walk through what the math looks

614
00:29:11,680 --> 00:29:14,300
like, we're going to assume we don't have any non-linearities,

615
00:29:14,300 --> 00:29:17,200
and all the bias terms are set to 0.

616
00:29:17,200 --> 00:29:21,760
So say we have h sub 1.

617
00:29:21,760 --> 00:29:25,060
And that's going to be equal to, like we said before, W

618
00:29:25,060 --> 00:29:32,340
of H sub 0 plus U of X sub 1.

619
00:29:32,340 --> 00:29:33,520
So H sub 1.

620
00:29:33,520 --> 00:29:35,333
The hidden state at that first step

621
00:29:35,333 --> 00:29:37,500
is going to be equal to the weights times the hidden

622
00:29:37,500 --> 00:29:40,580
state of the previous step plus these weights

623
00:29:40,580 --> 00:29:42,540
times the new input.

624
00:29:42,540 --> 00:29:45,020
Now, if we write out what this recurrence looks like-- now,

625
00:29:45,020 --> 00:29:47,020
if we say H sub 2, now that's going

626
00:29:47,020 --> 00:30:00,860
to be equal to W of W of H sub 0 plus U of X sub 1 plus U of X

627
00:30:00,860 --> 00:30:01,640
sub 2.

628
00:30:01,640 --> 00:30:05,020
So what do you notice here?

629
00:30:05,020 --> 00:30:08,980
Now, we have a W squared term essentially.

630
00:30:08,980 --> 00:30:12,460
And so now, if you do it for another time step,

631
00:30:12,460 --> 00:30:16,660
you're going to have W cubed, et cetera, all the way up to W

632
00:30:16,660 --> 00:30:19,540
to the N.

633
00:30:19,540 --> 00:30:23,290
So if you have this quadratic relationship with your weights

634
00:30:23,290 --> 00:30:28,250
matrix, what happens for values in that weight

635
00:30:28,250 --> 00:30:29,550
matrix that are small?

636
00:30:32,450 --> 00:30:34,030
They'll go to 0.

637
00:30:34,030 --> 00:30:37,890
And anything in that weight matrix that's big

638
00:30:37,890 --> 00:30:39,450
will be infinite.

639
00:30:39,450 --> 00:30:41,730
So what happens is in order for this

640
00:30:41,730 --> 00:30:45,330
to be stable at all during training,

641
00:30:45,330 --> 00:30:46,970
all the values kind of need to be

642
00:30:46,970 --> 00:30:51,090
close to 1, which means it's kind of hard to capture really

643
00:30:51,090 --> 00:30:52,770
variable information in there.

644
00:30:52,770 --> 00:30:55,150
And then additionally, no matter what,

645
00:30:55,150 --> 00:30:57,330
even if you're just a little bit below 1,

646
00:30:57,330 --> 00:30:58,930
when you go to infinity, it will go

647
00:30:58,930 --> 00:31:04,250
to 0 because you have this sort of exponential relationship.

648
00:31:04,250 --> 00:31:06,090
And so what this means is, often,

649
00:31:06,090 --> 00:31:08,330
that old observations in a recurrent neural network

650
00:31:08,330 --> 00:31:13,130
are forgotten, and that the stochastic gradients

651
00:31:13,130 --> 00:31:14,888
become really high variance.

652
00:31:14,888 --> 00:31:16,930
And you can either have these vanishing gradients

653
00:31:16,930 --> 00:31:18,730
or exploding gradients.

654
00:31:18,730 --> 00:31:24,610
And so this is why we started to look towards LSTMs.

655
00:31:24,610 --> 00:31:25,110
Yeah?

656
00:31:25,110 --> 00:31:26,670
AUDIENCE: So a couple lectures ago,

657
00:31:26,670 --> 00:31:28,430
we talked about the spectral norm

658
00:31:28,430 --> 00:31:32,210
and using that to normalize weights and activations.

659
00:31:32,210 --> 00:31:35,390
So I'm wondering, would that fix this problem?

660
00:31:35,390 --> 00:31:38,030
And if not, why not?

661
00:31:38,030 --> 00:31:40,230
SARA BEERY: So the question was, we previously

662
00:31:40,230 --> 00:31:42,950
talked about the spectral norm And using

663
00:31:42,950 --> 00:31:46,270
that as a way to try to mitigate things like these vanishing

664
00:31:46,270 --> 00:31:48,870
and exploding gradients.

665
00:31:48,870 --> 00:31:53,030
So you could try to build something in here that

666
00:31:53,030 --> 00:31:57,710
would try to normalize these weights so

667
00:31:57,710 --> 00:32:06,270
that they would be, again, close to 1, essentially, or not

668
00:32:06,270 --> 00:32:07,810
go to infinity or not.

669
00:32:07,810 --> 00:32:11,230
But this function is--

670
00:32:11,230 --> 00:32:15,270
like, this part here, the fact that because of the recurrence

671
00:32:15,270 --> 00:32:17,510
relationship, you're taking an exponential

672
00:32:17,510 --> 00:32:20,810
in the weights matrix.

673
00:32:20,810 --> 00:32:23,822
You could take a spectral norm over that exponential,

674
00:32:23,822 --> 00:32:26,030
but you're still going to take the exponential first.

675
00:32:26,030 --> 00:32:28,785
So that might help you with the exploding gradients problem,

676
00:32:28,785 --> 00:32:31,410
but it's not going to change the fact that the small things are

677
00:32:31,410 --> 00:32:33,050
going to go to 0.

678
00:32:33,050 --> 00:32:34,250
Does that make sense?

679
00:32:34,250 --> 00:32:34,990
Yeah, cool.

680
00:32:37,930 --> 00:32:39,130
And actually, if you--

681
00:32:39,130 --> 00:32:42,210
I thought this was kind of a fun blog post

682
00:32:42,210 --> 00:32:43,810
that specifically takes--

683
00:32:43,810 --> 00:32:45,830
it's from a control theory professor,

684
00:32:45,830 --> 00:32:50,930
but they're looking at stability analysis in recursion.

685
00:32:50,930 --> 00:32:53,890
And so if anyone's interested in the different ways

686
00:32:53,890 --> 00:32:56,850
that people think about what makes things stable or not

687
00:32:56,850 --> 00:33:00,390
stable in a system if you have recursion,

688
00:33:00,390 --> 00:33:04,770
I thought this was kind of a nice blog post.

689
00:33:04,770 --> 00:33:05,390
Yes?

690
00:33:05,390 --> 00:33:08,090
AUDIENCE: So you said gradients can explode or vanish.

691
00:33:08,090 --> 00:33:11,930
Do they both correspond to old memories being forgotten?

692
00:33:11,930 --> 00:33:14,810
And also as a follow-up, isn't it not a terrible thing

693
00:33:14,810 --> 00:33:19,390
that old memories are forgotten, because that's what humans do?

694
00:33:19,390 --> 00:33:22,110
SARA BEERY: So the question is, if you have exploding

695
00:33:22,110 --> 00:33:23,790
and vanishing, do they both correspond

696
00:33:23,790 --> 00:33:25,350
to memories of being forgotten?

697
00:33:25,350 --> 00:33:27,590
And no, the exploding ones correspond

698
00:33:27,590 --> 00:33:30,990
to unstable training, which we also don't really want.

699
00:33:30,990 --> 00:33:34,150
And the vanishing ones would be old memories being forgotten.

700
00:33:34,150 --> 00:33:38,190
And then do we actually want to remember things

701
00:33:38,190 --> 00:33:39,490
over long time horizons?

702
00:33:39,490 --> 00:33:42,550
And that's kind of the universal question-- do we need this?

703
00:33:42,550 --> 00:33:49,230
And I would say that there are definitely cases where we don't.

704
00:33:49,230 --> 00:33:51,930
And then there maybe are some cases where we do.

705
00:33:51,930 --> 00:33:56,150
So an example might be--

706
00:33:56,150 --> 00:33:57,830
think about temperature.

707
00:33:57,830 --> 00:34:00,850
So we have these daily cycles of temperature.

708
00:34:00,850 --> 00:34:03,408
So we'd like to remember that the temperature got warmer

709
00:34:03,408 --> 00:34:05,450
in the middle of the day than at night yesterday.

710
00:34:05,450 --> 00:34:06,710
So if we're looking at predicting temperature

711
00:34:06,710 --> 00:34:08,670
on some of fine-grained horizon, you'd

712
00:34:08,670 --> 00:34:11,333
want to be able to remember at least periodically a day back.

713
00:34:11,333 --> 00:34:13,750
But then also you have these yearly cycles of temperature,

714
00:34:13,750 --> 00:34:16,672
where it's hotter in the summer and cooler in the winter.

715
00:34:16,672 --> 00:34:18,630
So maybe if you're trying to model temperature,

716
00:34:18,630 --> 00:34:21,350
you also want to have this yearly dependency.

717
00:34:21,350 --> 00:34:23,570
And then you have things like these weather cycles,

718
00:34:23,570 --> 00:34:25,510
like El Niño, that happen every seven years.

719
00:34:25,510 --> 00:34:27,949
And so depending on what you're trying to model,

720
00:34:27,949 --> 00:34:29,730
there may actually be a lot of value

721
00:34:29,730 --> 00:34:31,889
in the longer and longer term memory

722
00:34:31,889 --> 00:34:34,810
if there's signal in more and more time scale.

723
00:34:34,810 --> 00:34:35,710
Does that make sense?

724
00:34:38,409 --> 00:34:38,909
Cool.

725
00:34:38,909 --> 00:34:41,929
So this is why we introduced LSTMs,

726
00:34:41,929 --> 00:34:44,210
which is to try to deal with forgetting,

727
00:34:44,210 --> 00:34:46,534
to try to deal with this vanishing gradient problem.

728
00:34:46,534 --> 00:34:48,409
And the reason the vanishing gradient problem

729
00:34:48,409 --> 00:34:51,650
is more of a problem than the unstable exploding gradients

730
00:34:51,650 --> 00:34:54,989
is exactly like what this guy over here mentioned.

731
00:34:54,989 --> 00:34:57,610
There are things we can do to try to force--

732
00:34:57,610 --> 00:34:59,450
to take normalization over the gradients

733
00:34:59,450 --> 00:35:01,610
to keep them from exploding-- different ways

734
00:35:01,610 --> 00:35:04,310
that we can constrain it, or regularize it, or clip it.

735
00:35:04,310 --> 00:35:09,530
But we can't do that when things go to 0.

736
00:35:09,530 --> 00:35:17,280
So LSTMs are essentially designed so that the default

737
00:35:17,280 --> 00:35:20,040
behavior is to not forget.

738
00:35:20,040 --> 00:35:23,400
And then you have to learn to forget.

739
00:35:23,400 --> 00:35:25,100
And so the default is the identity.

740
00:35:25,100 --> 00:35:27,360
You're biased to not forget anything.

741
00:35:27,360 --> 00:35:29,860
And then you learn what to forget,

742
00:35:29,860 --> 00:35:32,360
kind of like garbage collection-- what to throw away.

743
00:35:32,360 --> 00:35:35,640
So that you can add new stuff.

744
00:35:35,640 --> 00:35:38,480
And so if you look at this-- so this

745
00:35:38,480 --> 00:35:42,580
is kind of like the standard recurrent neural network.

746
00:35:42,580 --> 00:35:44,280
We have some non-linearity.

747
00:35:44,280 --> 00:35:46,160
This is kind of a simplified version

748
00:35:46,160 --> 00:35:48,220
where it looks like we're sending in the inputs.

749
00:35:48,220 --> 00:35:50,100
There's a function here that's mapping this in.

750
00:35:50,100 --> 00:35:51,660
And then we're sending out some hidden state,

751
00:35:51,660 --> 00:35:53,200
but then there'd be some function mapping that

752
00:35:53,200 --> 00:35:54,100
to the output.

753
00:35:54,100 --> 00:35:56,560
We're just only looking at the hidden layer

754
00:35:56,560 --> 00:35:58,960
component of this model.

755
00:35:58,960 --> 00:36:03,200
And so what an LSTM does is it builds a little memory

756
00:36:03,200 --> 00:36:07,760
controller that decides what to save and what to delete.

757
00:36:07,760 --> 00:36:10,420
And it turns out this is still Turing complete.

758
00:36:10,420 --> 00:36:12,780
And it's actually very close to a Turing machine.

759
00:36:12,780 --> 00:36:14,820
It's kind of like that idea of a hidden tape

760
00:36:14,820 --> 00:36:16,940
that you can read to or write from.

761
00:36:16,940 --> 00:36:20,180
And so now, every single one of these functions

762
00:36:20,180 --> 00:36:22,700
that maps us from our inputs to what

763
00:36:22,700 --> 00:36:25,660
the next hidden state should be has this little controller

764
00:36:25,660 --> 00:36:27,340
built in.

765
00:36:27,340 --> 00:36:29,860
And so let's go through the different components

766
00:36:29,860 --> 00:36:31,300
of this thing.

767
00:36:31,300 --> 00:36:34,540
So first, we have this cell state C. This is a new thing

768
00:36:34,540 --> 00:36:36,500
that we're introducing for an LSTM.

769
00:36:36,500 --> 00:36:39,380
And this is kind of the tape in the Turing machine.

770
00:36:39,380 --> 00:36:42,900
And this cell state is what gets modulated by the part that

771
00:36:42,900 --> 00:36:46,420
decides what to forget and what to add.

772
00:36:46,420 --> 00:36:51,620
So then we'll have some function, f of t.

773
00:36:51,620 --> 00:36:56,460
And this function you'll operate over,

774
00:36:56,460 --> 00:36:59,900
applying your previous weights to the previous hidden state,

775
00:36:59,900 --> 00:37:03,220
and your current state, and then your biases.

776
00:37:03,220 --> 00:37:07,100
And that is going to be non-linearized

777
00:37:07,100 --> 00:37:08,400
by something like a sigmoid.

778
00:37:08,400 --> 00:37:10,580
So if it's large, it goes to 1.

779
00:37:10,580 --> 00:37:12,820
And if it's small, it goes to 0.

780
00:37:12,820 --> 00:37:15,880
And so now, if you're taking this thing--

781
00:37:15,880 --> 00:37:20,200
now, if you multiply by 0-- so if this value ends up

782
00:37:20,200 --> 00:37:23,240
being small coming out of the function,

783
00:37:23,240 --> 00:37:25,460
then it's telling you forget this thing.

784
00:37:28,280 --> 00:37:30,480
So what you want to do is give high values

785
00:37:30,480 --> 00:37:33,600
to things you want to remember and low values

786
00:37:33,600 --> 00:37:35,840
to things you want to forget.

787
00:37:35,840 --> 00:37:38,480
So that's like what this component of the LCM

788
00:37:38,480 --> 00:37:42,760
is in charge of, deciding what to remember and what to forget.

789
00:37:42,760 --> 00:37:47,400
And then each element of that cell state, essentially

790
00:37:47,400 --> 00:37:49,553
because of the way that we're using

791
00:37:49,553 --> 00:37:51,220
a sigmoid and not something like a ReLU,

792
00:37:51,220 --> 00:37:53,960
which would be unbounded, is-- it's essentially

793
00:37:53,960 --> 00:37:56,120
the identity function with some masking of what

794
00:37:56,120 --> 00:37:59,120
we want to forget.

795
00:37:59,120 --> 00:38:02,560
So then there's this next component.

796
00:38:02,560 --> 00:38:04,400
And this one is determining which

797
00:38:04,400 --> 00:38:08,160
indices we want to write to and what to write.

798
00:38:08,160 --> 00:38:13,420
So now, we have essentially this i

799
00:38:13,420 --> 00:38:16,380
that is used to determine basically which indices

800
00:38:16,380 --> 00:38:17,460
we're going to write to.

801
00:38:17,460 --> 00:38:21,780
And then the C of t is going to be

802
00:38:21,780 --> 00:38:25,240
how that information kind of gets added to the cell state.

803
00:38:29,220 --> 00:38:34,580
And then, finally, we need to do the actual forgetting.

804
00:38:34,580 --> 00:38:38,100
And so this is where this comes in.

805
00:38:38,100 --> 00:38:40,300
What's quite different is, for the first time now,

806
00:38:40,300 --> 00:38:43,020
we have these multiplicative relationships right.

807
00:38:43,020 --> 00:38:44,280
So you're multiplying.

808
00:38:44,280 --> 00:38:45,863
You're taking the previous cell state,

809
00:38:45,863 --> 00:38:47,500
and you're multiplying it by this f sub

810
00:38:47,500 --> 00:38:50,060
t, which is going to be kind of either ones or 0's, or very

811
00:38:50,060 --> 00:38:51,300
close to.

812
00:38:51,300 --> 00:38:54,780
And then so now that's kind of updating the cell

813
00:38:54,780 --> 00:38:55,920
state in the first place.

814
00:38:55,920 --> 00:38:59,440
And then you're going to add this new information.

815
00:38:59,440 --> 00:39:04,020
So you now have this i sub t multiplied by the new cell state

816
00:39:04,020 --> 00:39:04,820
C sub t.

817
00:39:04,820 --> 00:39:07,500
So in this case, it's removing information

818
00:39:07,500 --> 00:39:10,570
and adding information.

819
00:39:10,570 --> 00:39:14,130
So now, after you've actually updated that information

820
00:39:14,130 --> 00:39:16,250
in the cell state, now we're going

821
00:39:16,250 --> 00:39:19,530
to decide what gets output to the next hidden state.

822
00:39:19,530 --> 00:39:21,970
It's not mathematically similar, but we just

823
00:39:21,970 --> 00:39:26,410
learned about transformers right where we have the keys,

824
00:39:26,410 --> 00:39:27,930
queries, and values.

825
00:39:27,930 --> 00:39:31,350
So in that case, when we talk about this value projection,

826
00:39:31,350 --> 00:39:34,370
these value matrix, that's kind of telling you

827
00:39:34,370 --> 00:39:36,970
how you should output that next state

828
00:39:36,970 --> 00:39:40,830
or how you should output the output of that thing.

829
00:39:40,830 --> 00:39:47,457
And this feels to me, on vibes, kind of like values.

830
00:39:47,457 --> 00:39:49,290
You've updated the cell states' information,

831
00:39:49,290 --> 00:39:53,250
and now you want to say, how do we take that and update it

832
00:39:53,250 --> 00:39:55,970
to the next hidden state?

833
00:39:55,970 --> 00:39:59,130
And then note-- so we have now--

834
00:39:59,130 --> 00:40:01,010
we have both these hidden states, which

835
00:40:01,010 --> 00:40:02,410
are getting passed through.

836
00:40:02,410 --> 00:40:05,143
But we also have that ticker tape, that cell state, that's

837
00:40:05,143 --> 00:40:06,310
also getting passed through.

838
00:40:06,310 --> 00:40:09,470
So there's kind of two things going on.

839
00:40:09,470 --> 00:40:10,710
Cool.

840
00:40:10,710 --> 00:40:12,390
So really, this important point--

841
00:40:12,390 --> 00:40:16,670
the important thing that this has beyond that vanilla RNN

842
00:40:16,670 --> 00:40:19,550
is the fact that you have almost this gating functionality that's

843
00:40:19,550 --> 00:40:22,710
provided by multiplicative updates to the cell state,

844
00:40:22,710 --> 00:40:24,830
where now we explicitly learn what to remember

845
00:40:24,830 --> 00:40:26,310
and what to forget.

846
00:40:26,310 --> 00:40:28,350
And then you multiply by 1, so you

847
00:40:28,350 --> 00:40:30,270
have this identity instead of multiplying

848
00:40:30,270 --> 00:40:33,230
by yourself, which removes the vanishing and exploding

849
00:40:33,230 --> 00:40:33,770
gradients.

850
00:40:33,770 --> 00:40:36,230
We no longer have that exponential.

851
00:40:36,230 --> 00:40:38,230
Instead, you're only ever going to do something

852
00:40:38,230 --> 00:40:41,330
like the identity or remove it and add something new.

853
00:40:45,090 --> 00:40:46,647
Because the default is the identity,

854
00:40:46,647 --> 00:40:48,230
it's kind of like a skipped connection

855
00:40:48,230 --> 00:40:50,570
or a residual connection in a ResNet.

856
00:40:50,570 --> 00:40:52,870
You have this component where you could just

857
00:40:52,870 --> 00:40:54,910
learn, throughout this entire thing,

858
00:40:54,910 --> 00:40:57,610
to only ever send the identity--

859
00:40:57,610 --> 00:41:01,790
just send the same information forward all the time.

860
00:41:01,790 --> 00:41:02,910
Awesome.

861
00:41:02,910 --> 00:41:05,070
Any other questions about LSTMs?

862
00:41:05,070 --> 00:41:07,910
This part is a little fiddly, so definitely

863
00:41:07,910 --> 00:41:13,090
feel free to go back through or visit this really nice blog post

864
00:41:13,090 --> 00:41:14,970
that we pulled some of these visualizations

865
00:41:14,970 --> 00:41:16,970
from, from Chris Olah.

866
00:41:16,970 --> 00:41:17,650
Yeah?

867
00:41:17,650 --> 00:41:19,570
AUDIENCE: It's, element-wise, multiplication

868
00:41:19,570 --> 00:41:22,090
for the hidden state.

869
00:41:22,090 --> 00:41:23,155
Is that correct?

870
00:41:23,155 --> 00:41:24,530
SARA BEERY: So the multiplication

871
00:41:24,530 --> 00:41:27,630
is basically like you're learning this gating function f

872
00:41:27,630 --> 00:41:29,570
that's telling you what to forget.

873
00:41:29,570 --> 00:41:31,450
And then you're learning this i that's

874
00:41:31,450 --> 00:41:35,510
telling you like what new information to add back in.

875
00:41:35,510 --> 00:41:37,873
And so those two things are going to be multiplied in.

876
00:41:37,873 --> 00:41:39,290
And they're going to be multiplied

877
00:41:39,290 --> 00:41:44,610
in element-wise in your C depending on what the size of C

878
00:41:44,610 --> 00:41:45,110
is.

879
00:41:48,490 --> 00:41:49,228
Yeah?

880
00:41:49,228 --> 00:41:49,895
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

881
00:41:49,895 --> 00:41:51,312
Sorry if you've already said this.

882
00:41:51,312 --> 00:41:53,390
So it's going to be like an MLP or something?

883
00:41:53,390 --> 00:41:54,330
SARA BEERY: Huh?

884
00:41:54,330 --> 00:41:56,050
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] MLP or--

885
00:41:56,050 --> 00:42:02,290
SARA BEERY: Oh, the sigmas are sigmoid functions--

886
00:42:02,290 --> 00:42:04,890
literally sigmoid functions.

887
00:42:04,890 --> 00:42:06,730
Yeah, and the reason that people often

888
00:42:06,730 --> 00:42:08,713
use-- you could use another non-linearity,

889
00:42:08,713 --> 00:42:10,630
but the reason you use a sigmoid function here

890
00:42:10,630 --> 00:42:13,030
is because it's bounded between 0 and 1.

891
00:42:13,030 --> 00:42:15,990
So you want large values to map to 1 and small values to map

892
00:42:15,990 --> 00:42:16,690
to 0.

893
00:42:16,690 --> 00:42:20,063
AUDIENCE: Oh, but the weight going to [INAUDIBLE] not

894
00:42:20,063 --> 00:42:20,730
on the diagonal.

895
00:42:20,730 --> 00:42:22,030
SARA BEERY: Yes.

896
00:42:22,030 --> 00:42:23,602
AUDIENCE: Makes sense.

897
00:42:23,602 --> 00:42:26,310
SARA BEERY: The question was, if the weights are on the diagonal,

898
00:42:26,310 --> 00:42:28,470
and the answer is no.

899
00:42:28,470 --> 00:42:30,870
Cool.

900
00:42:30,870 --> 00:42:32,510
Let's talk about sequence models.

901
00:42:32,510 --> 00:42:36,310
So we just talked about a bunch of different ways

902
00:42:36,310 --> 00:42:41,630
that you can think about trying to model sequences.

903
00:42:41,630 --> 00:42:48,030
But there's this new kid in town of what we

904
00:42:48,030 --> 00:42:50,710
call an autoregressive model.

905
00:42:50,710 --> 00:42:53,350
So previously, we said, we're going

906
00:42:53,350 --> 00:42:54,690
to have some fixed time window.

907
00:42:54,690 --> 00:42:56,607
We're going to be sending this memory forward.

908
00:42:56,607 --> 00:42:58,030
Things could be infinitely long.

909
00:42:58,030 --> 00:42:59,910
But the idea of an autoregressive model

910
00:42:59,910 --> 00:43:02,870
is quite simple, and we've introduced them before.

911
00:43:02,870 --> 00:43:06,850
So the idea is you have some beginning of a sequence,

912
00:43:06,850 --> 00:43:09,730
and then you ask it to predict what comes next.

913
00:43:09,730 --> 00:43:12,410
And then this is the autoregressive part.

914
00:43:12,410 --> 00:43:13,970
You add that in.

915
00:43:13,970 --> 00:43:16,950
And now, again, you ask to predict what comes next.

916
00:43:16,950 --> 00:43:22,670
And this idea of continually predicting what's next,

917
00:43:22,670 --> 00:43:25,410
predicting what's next, this is how

918
00:43:25,410 --> 00:43:29,530
we think about doing modern generative AI.

919
00:43:29,530 --> 00:43:31,930
A lot of these things like ChatGPT,

920
00:43:31,930 --> 00:43:33,850
these language models will explicitly

921
00:43:33,850 --> 00:43:36,192
be trained to be autoregressive.

922
00:43:36,192 --> 00:43:37,650
So of course, there's no reason why

923
00:43:37,650 --> 00:43:39,850
you can't take the same framework

924
00:43:39,850 --> 00:43:45,130
and instead tell it to fill in the blanks or something.

925
00:43:45,130 --> 00:43:48,330
So when you're actually training one

926
00:43:48,330 --> 00:43:51,850
of these autoregressive models, you

927
00:43:51,850 --> 00:43:55,050
might have a bunch of different examples of sentences,

928
00:43:55,050 --> 00:43:58,330
and then the beginnings of sentences

929
00:43:58,330 --> 00:43:59,430
and then the next word.

930
00:43:59,430 --> 00:44:01,570
And so then the learner has to learn

931
00:44:01,570 --> 00:44:05,320
from the beginning of a sentence what is the next word.

932
00:44:05,320 --> 00:44:08,480
And then when you're actually sampling,

933
00:44:08,480 --> 00:44:10,960
you would do something like send in some new beginning

934
00:44:10,960 --> 00:44:15,640
of a sentence, and then use your predictor until you want to stop

935
00:44:15,640 --> 00:44:17,400
or until the predictor decides to stop

936
00:44:17,400 --> 00:44:18,780
to predict the next thing.

937
00:44:18,780 --> 00:44:19,480
Yeah?

938
00:44:19,480 --> 00:44:23,780
AUDIENCE: You mean you try-- predict something at time t?

939
00:44:23,780 --> 00:44:28,920
I take that prediction and pass that through [INAUDIBLE]?

940
00:44:28,920 --> 00:44:31,560
SARA BEERY: So now, you predict something at time t

941
00:44:31,560 --> 00:44:33,660
And then you take that prediction,

942
00:44:33,660 --> 00:44:35,780
and you add it to now what's your input.

943
00:44:35,780 --> 00:44:39,160
So now, your input is everything up to t minus 1

944
00:44:39,160 --> 00:44:41,160
and your prediction for t.

945
00:44:41,160 --> 00:44:44,240
And then you ask it to predict t plus 1, and then you add it in.

946
00:44:44,240 --> 00:44:46,840
AUDIENCE: And this is what you mean by sampling?

947
00:44:46,840 --> 00:44:48,340
Is that why you used the word there?

948
00:44:48,340 --> 00:44:50,640
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so then actually, the sampling part--

949
00:44:50,640 --> 00:44:52,348
this is that's exactly what would happen.

950
00:44:52,348 --> 00:44:56,640
You would send in as your input colorless, green, ideas, sleep.

951
00:44:56,640 --> 00:45:00,320
Ask your predictor what comes next-- says furiously.

952
00:45:00,320 --> 00:45:02,080
And then you would take that furiously,

953
00:45:02,080 --> 00:45:02,960
and you could add it into.

954
00:45:02,960 --> 00:45:05,043
And you could say, well, what if I said colorless,

955
00:45:05,043 --> 00:45:07,620
green, ideas, sleep, furiously-- then what comes next?

956
00:45:07,620 --> 00:45:10,620
So this is this idea of autoregression,

957
00:45:10,620 --> 00:45:12,893
that you're using your predictions,

958
00:45:12,893 --> 00:45:15,060
adding them to the input, and then predicting again,

959
00:45:15,060 --> 00:45:16,160
and again, and again.

960
00:45:16,160 --> 00:45:19,700
AUDIENCE: Is that [INAUDIBLE] Is that one

961
00:45:19,700 --> 00:45:24,180
way you could evaluate an RNN-- just print out these samples

962
00:45:24,180 --> 00:45:25,757
and see what it produces?

963
00:45:25,757 --> 00:45:27,340
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so I mean, you could

964
00:45:27,340 --> 00:45:31,360
think of using an RNN to predict the next word.

965
00:45:31,360 --> 00:45:34,980
So your output would be what we think the next word might be.

966
00:45:34,980 --> 00:45:40,700
But what an RNN doesn't have is this explicit loop back

967
00:45:40,700 --> 00:45:44,100
where now that output from up to t plus 1

968
00:45:44,100 --> 00:45:48,045
becomes the part of the input at the next time step.

969
00:45:48,045 --> 00:45:48,920
Does that make sense?

970
00:45:48,920 --> 00:45:51,378
So it's not innately something that you would do in an RNN,

971
00:45:51,378 --> 00:45:54,120
but you could totally loop it around that way.

972
00:45:54,120 --> 00:45:56,400
And kind of construct it.

973
00:45:56,400 --> 00:45:56,900
Yeah?

974
00:45:56,900 --> 00:45:58,820
AUDIENCE: So with this model, the predictor

975
00:45:58,820 --> 00:46:01,880
outputs sort of a distribution, and then you

976
00:46:01,880 --> 00:46:05,640
sample one element from this distribution, I guess.

977
00:46:05,640 --> 00:46:06,760
But what if you--

978
00:46:06,760 --> 00:46:09,440
[CHUCKLES]

979
00:46:09,440 --> 00:46:10,800
SARA BEERY: Anyway, continue.

980
00:46:10,800 --> 00:46:13,720
AUDIENCE: What if, instead of sampling one

981
00:46:13,720 --> 00:46:16,880
and adding it to the input, what if you put the distribution

982
00:46:16,880 --> 00:46:18,775
in the input?

983
00:46:18,775 --> 00:46:20,900
SARA BEERY: Yeah, you're just begging the question.

984
00:46:20,900 --> 00:46:23,600
We're about to get into the probability models.

985
00:46:23,600 --> 00:46:26,200
But the question was basically what

986
00:46:26,200 --> 00:46:29,480
we said-- what I just said was, you take the prediction,

987
00:46:29,480 --> 00:46:31,120
and maybe that would be a distribution,

988
00:46:31,120 --> 00:46:33,120
and you're picking the maximum, and then

989
00:46:33,120 --> 00:46:34,493
you put that maximum in.

990
00:46:34,493 --> 00:46:36,160
What if instead you put the distribution

991
00:46:36,160 --> 00:46:38,620
of possible next options in?

992
00:46:38,620 --> 00:46:40,120
And we'll talk about different ways

993
00:46:40,120 --> 00:46:41,700
that people think about doing that.

994
00:46:44,760 --> 00:46:48,572
So essentially, what we're talking about is--

995
00:46:48,572 --> 00:46:50,280
or one way that you can think about doing

996
00:46:50,280 --> 00:46:55,220
this is trying to learn some probability of the next word.

997
00:46:55,220 --> 00:47:00,480
And you can take that and factorize the probability

998
00:47:00,480 --> 00:47:03,020
of that joint distribution.

999
00:47:03,020 --> 00:47:04,660
Basically, you're just reformulating

1000
00:47:04,660 --> 00:47:07,140
the conditional distribution-- the probability

1001
00:47:07,140 --> 00:47:10,963
of the next word given everything we've seen before.

1002
00:47:10,963 --> 00:47:12,880
This is true for any probability distribution.

1003
00:47:12,880 --> 00:47:14,420
You factorize them out, and you get

1004
00:47:14,420 --> 00:47:22,900
something that's multiplicative and is somewhat still

1005
00:47:22,900 --> 00:47:24,940
autoregressive, because you're taking

1006
00:47:24,940 --> 00:47:26,820
the probability of every previous thing

1007
00:47:26,820 --> 00:47:28,740
up until that point.

1008
00:47:28,740 --> 00:47:36,540
So you can think of this-- the sequence models in transformers

1009
00:47:36,540 --> 00:47:39,180
when we talked about it in the transformer lecture last week--

1010
00:47:39,180 --> 00:47:42,440
as modeling for example, the probability of a sentence.

1011
00:47:42,440 --> 00:47:43,960
And then that could be generative.

1012
00:47:43,960 --> 00:47:46,998
So here what this looks like in practice.

1013
00:47:46,998 --> 00:47:49,540
So say you're trying to look at the probability of "once upon

1014
00:47:49,540 --> 00:47:50,900
a time."

1015
00:47:50,900 --> 00:47:52,940
That's the same as the probability of "once--"

1016
00:47:52,940 --> 00:47:56,060
the probability of starting with the first word "once--"

1017
00:47:56,060 --> 00:47:59,160
times the probability of getting "upon,"

1018
00:47:59,160 --> 00:48:01,240
given that your first word was "once,"

1019
00:48:01,240 --> 00:48:04,440
times the probability that you'd get "a" given that you already

1020
00:48:04,440 --> 00:48:08,560
had "once upon," and then, et cetera, et cetera.

1021
00:48:08,560 --> 00:48:12,880
So this is how you would model the probability of given

1022
00:48:12,880 --> 00:48:16,853
sentence-- the likelihood of a sentence.

1023
00:48:16,853 --> 00:48:20,800
So what's a function that we could

1024
00:48:20,800 --> 00:48:24,920
use that would map the probability of the next word

1025
00:48:24,920 --> 00:48:27,100
given the previous word?

1026
00:48:29,680 --> 00:48:33,880
Maybe some of the things that we've talked about in class--

1027
00:48:33,880 --> 00:48:35,420
specifically the probability.

1028
00:48:43,760 --> 00:48:44,955
Yeah, go ahead?

1029
00:48:44,955 --> 00:48:47,080
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] regression or some deep thing

1030
00:48:47,080 --> 00:48:49,407
going into logistic regression?

1031
00:48:49,407 --> 00:48:51,240
SARA BEERY: So you said logistic regression.

1032
00:48:51,240 --> 00:48:51,990
AUDIENCE: Softmax.

1033
00:48:51,990 --> 00:48:53,500
SARA BEERY: A softmax-- oh, yeah.

1034
00:48:53,500 --> 00:48:55,200
So softmax takes an input and then

1035
00:48:55,200 --> 00:48:57,080
gives us something that might not

1036
00:48:57,080 --> 00:48:59,530
be a probability distribution, but at least sums to 1.

1037
00:48:59,530 --> 00:49:04,790
So what is one of the ways we frequently use the softmax?

1038
00:49:04,790 --> 00:49:05,668
When we're doing--

1039
00:49:05,668 --> 00:49:06,710
AUDIENCE: Classification.

1040
00:49:06,710 --> 00:49:07,835
SARA BEERY: Classification.

1041
00:49:07,835 --> 00:49:08,990
Yes, exactly.

1042
00:49:08,990 --> 00:49:12,130
So we could just treat this as a next-word classifier.

1043
00:49:12,130 --> 00:49:14,190
And we I think we talked in the hacker lecture

1044
00:49:14,190 --> 00:49:17,223
about how a reasonable starting point for a lot of things

1045
00:49:17,223 --> 00:49:19,390
is just being like, let's see if we can formulate it

1046
00:49:19,390 --> 00:49:21,430
as a multi-class classifier.

1047
00:49:21,430 --> 00:49:24,410
So here, then the input is "once upon a."

1048
00:49:24,410 --> 00:49:27,710
And then you learn a next word classifier

1049
00:49:27,710 --> 00:49:29,910
that would probably, by taking a softmax,

1050
00:49:29,910 --> 00:49:32,430
give us some distribution over the

1051
00:49:32,430 --> 00:49:34,050
likelihood of the next words.

1052
00:49:34,050 --> 00:49:39,470
And so now, here, a standard multi-class classifier

1053
00:49:39,470 --> 00:49:41,030
will use some neural net.

1054
00:49:41,030 --> 00:49:43,830
That softmax will squish the outputs into a probability mass

1055
00:49:43,830 --> 00:49:45,990
function.

1056
00:49:45,990 --> 00:49:48,150
Non-negative vector sums to 1.

1057
00:49:48,150 --> 00:49:53,270
And then you learn that by penalizing it via cross-entropy.

1058
00:49:53,270 --> 00:49:55,370
So how would you map words to classes?

1059
00:49:55,370 --> 00:49:57,390
How many classes would there be?

1060
00:50:00,010 --> 00:50:01,610
Lots.

1061
00:50:01,610 --> 00:50:04,970
So if you use words and u-- every possible word

1062
00:50:04,970 --> 00:50:07,650
in the English language, which there are many more words

1063
00:50:07,650 --> 00:50:09,113
in some other languages on Earth,

1064
00:50:09,113 --> 00:50:11,530
and you represent all those words as 1-hot-vectors of size

1065
00:50:11,530 --> 00:50:15,010
k, k would be about 100,000.

1066
00:50:15,010 --> 00:50:17,010
And we know that multi-class classification

1067
00:50:17,010 --> 00:50:22,010
starts to be a lot less easy to learn when the number of classes

1068
00:50:22,010 --> 00:50:23,350
starts to scale quite large.

1069
00:50:23,350 --> 00:50:27,490
So it is possible, for example, like we run some 100,000

1070
00:50:27,490 --> 00:50:30,270
classifiers for species in iNaturalist.

1071
00:50:30,270 --> 00:50:33,690
There's about 450,000 species currently in iNaturalist.

1072
00:50:33,690 --> 00:50:35,610
We train classifiers for all of them,

1073
00:50:35,610 --> 00:50:39,450
but you need a lot of data.

1074
00:50:39,450 --> 00:50:43,010
And it can be quite unstable.

1075
00:50:43,010 --> 00:50:48,370
So instead, another thing that you could do

1076
00:50:48,370 --> 00:50:53,890
is you could represent every character as a class.

1077
00:50:53,890 --> 00:50:56,870
So now, in the English language, the alphabet is 26.

1078
00:50:56,870 --> 00:50:58,950
So now, we only have k equals 26.

1079
00:50:58,950 --> 00:51:01,910
And that's a really reasonable set of classes.

1080
00:51:01,910 --> 00:51:05,190
But the thing is, now your classification is fewer way,

1081
00:51:05,190 --> 00:51:07,665
but the sequence prediction is a lot harder.

1082
00:51:07,665 --> 00:51:09,290
You have to take a lot more time steps.

1083
00:51:09,290 --> 00:51:11,510
There's a lot more predictions that you have to take.

1084
00:51:11,510 --> 00:51:16,810
And it's pretty easy for these things to devolve over time.

1085
00:51:16,810 --> 00:51:21,990
Imagine it starts spelling something weird, and then

1086
00:51:21,990 --> 00:51:24,470
where are you going to go?

1087
00:51:24,470 --> 00:51:27,750
So ideally, you'd want something somewhat in between.

1088
00:51:27,750 --> 00:51:31,630
And it turns out, in practice, that what's kind of a sweet spot

1089
00:51:31,630 --> 00:51:39,470
is this idea of byte pairs, which is 2 character pairs.

1090
00:51:39,470 --> 00:51:42,770
And so now, you represent words.

1091
00:51:42,770 --> 00:51:46,310
It's about 1,000-- 26 times 26, maybe plus a few more--

1092
00:51:46,310 --> 00:51:48,190
of these byte pairs.

1093
00:51:48,190 --> 00:51:50,390
These tokens of these two character pairs

1094
00:51:50,390 --> 00:51:53,870
is quite frequent, even in the largest scale language

1095
00:51:53,870 --> 00:51:56,570
models we have.

1096
00:51:56,570 --> 00:51:57,850
Cool.

1097
00:51:57,850 --> 00:52:03,810
So let's talk about then what this might look like.

1098
00:52:03,810 --> 00:52:07,770
So you have this kind of probability, this classifier

1099
00:52:07,770 --> 00:52:10,050
for the next step, basically.

1100
00:52:10,050 --> 00:52:13,730
And then you send your output prediction forward.

1101
00:52:13,730 --> 00:52:15,670
So one example of this--

1102
00:52:15,670 --> 00:52:19,610
this is an example that's taking in some caffeine molecule.

1103
00:52:19,610 --> 00:52:21,890
And then you have a model that's trying to output

1104
00:52:21,890 --> 00:52:24,690
a description of that molecule.

1105
00:52:24,690 --> 00:52:27,630
So maybe that would look something like stimulant--

1106
00:52:27,630 --> 00:52:33,090
"a mild stimulant that enhances cognitive ability."

1107
00:52:33,090 --> 00:52:35,868
So you use this caffeine molecule.

1108
00:52:35,868 --> 00:52:37,410
You have some representation that you

1109
00:52:37,410 --> 00:52:41,830
use to start your word generator somewhere,

1110
00:52:41,830 --> 00:52:43,550
and then you would describe this.

1111
00:52:43,550 --> 00:52:46,530
Maybe this we would call a molecule to text.

1112
00:52:46,530 --> 00:52:51,050
So one way you could do this would be something like an LSTM.

1113
00:52:51,050 --> 00:52:53,850
So now, you have your molecule.

1114
00:52:53,850 --> 00:52:55,600
You run it through some GNN that gives you

1115
00:52:55,600 --> 00:52:57,475
some representation of that molecule that you

1116
00:52:57,475 --> 00:53:00,035
can use to condition on your starting point.

1117
00:53:00,035 --> 00:53:01,660
Then, once you have the starting point,

1118
00:53:01,660 --> 00:53:05,980
the LSTM is just predicting that next word at any given point.

1119
00:53:05,980 --> 00:53:11,240
And you're taking the previous things into account.

1120
00:53:11,240 --> 00:53:15,960
So if you're actually looking to train this, now,

1121
00:53:15,960 --> 00:53:19,280
the training data is something like "a mild stimulant that

1122
00:53:19,280 --> 00:53:22,425
enhances cognitive ability," end character-- stop predicting.

1123
00:53:22,425 --> 00:53:23,800
So now, for the first time, we've

1124
00:53:23,800 --> 00:53:27,720
introduced the idea of stopping when you're done.

1125
00:53:27,720 --> 00:53:29,840
And then your outputs would maybe

1126
00:53:29,840 --> 00:53:33,560
be some probability distribution or likelihood

1127
00:53:33,560 --> 00:53:35,968
over those next words given the previous words.

1128
00:53:35,968 --> 00:53:37,760
And then you could train it using something

1129
00:53:37,760 --> 00:53:39,343
like a max likelihood objective, where

1130
00:53:39,343 --> 00:53:42,040
you want to maximize the probability the model assigns

1131
00:53:42,040 --> 00:53:43,340
to each target word.

1132
00:53:43,340 --> 00:53:47,560
You want it to maximize the probability of the word that

1133
00:53:47,560 --> 00:53:49,260
was actually in the sentence.

1134
00:53:55,740 --> 00:53:57,440
So now, you have your targets.

1135
00:53:57,440 --> 00:54:00,135
You 1-hot encode them, and then you can do something like

1136
00:54:00,135 --> 00:54:02,260
maximizing cross-entropy, because now you have this

1137
00:54:02,260 --> 00:54:03,480
multi-class classification.

1138
00:54:03,480 --> 00:54:06,580
You can train it the same way we've trained things before.

1139
00:54:06,580 --> 00:54:09,380
Now, one thing you might actually

1140
00:54:09,380 --> 00:54:11,700
want to do in training-- currently,

1141
00:54:11,700 --> 00:54:14,180
we're saying you would take your prediction,

1142
00:54:14,180 --> 00:54:17,500
and then you would use that and send it forward

1143
00:54:17,500 --> 00:54:20,940
to the next thing as the previous word.

1144
00:54:20,940 --> 00:54:22,480
And you're learning from that.

1145
00:54:22,480 --> 00:54:24,340
But actually, in training, often, we

1146
00:54:24,340 --> 00:54:27,700
use something called teacher forcing, which is basically,

1147
00:54:27,700 --> 00:54:30,320
even if you predict the wrong thing at a given time step,

1148
00:54:30,320 --> 00:54:34,100
you enforce the correct word goes in as the input.

1149
00:54:34,100 --> 00:54:39,180
So you actually send this in now and condition each next word

1150
00:54:39,180 --> 00:54:42,220
prediction on the ground-truth preceding words instead

1151
00:54:42,220 --> 00:54:45,140
of letting it devolve and then penalizing it

1152
00:54:45,140 --> 00:54:49,220
for every mistake it made after it made its first mistake.

1153
00:54:49,220 --> 00:54:52,040
And then at test time, what you would do

1154
00:54:52,040 --> 00:54:53,820
is now you have your starting point,

1155
00:54:53,820 --> 00:54:56,445
and then you're going to sample from the predicted distribution

1156
00:54:56,445 --> 00:54:57,480
over those words.

1157
00:54:57,480 --> 00:54:59,418
So for example, "a."

1158
00:54:59,418 --> 00:55:00,960
Now, we do that autoregressive thing,

1159
00:55:00,960 --> 00:55:04,800
where we send that in, and then we predict the next thing.

1160
00:55:04,800 --> 00:55:10,360
"A strong stimulant that enhances cognitive ability."

1161
00:55:10,360 --> 00:55:16,320
But here, you'll note that after "a," we weren't super sure what

1162
00:55:16,320 --> 00:55:17,340
was likely next.

1163
00:55:17,340 --> 00:55:19,840
And so the model predicted "strong."

1164
00:55:19,840 --> 00:55:25,620
And it turns out that "strong" was exactly wrong.

1165
00:55:25,620 --> 00:55:28,540
We said "mild" originally.

1166
00:55:28,540 --> 00:55:31,080
And that actually is pretty different in terms

1167
00:55:31,080 --> 00:55:33,040
of the meaning of that sentence.

1168
00:55:33,040 --> 00:55:34,562
And so this is something where you

1169
00:55:34,562 --> 00:55:36,520
have this autoregressive modeling-- if you make

1170
00:55:36,520 --> 00:55:39,240
one mistake, then often, it can take you

1171
00:55:39,240 --> 00:55:41,000
down a path in almost this tree where

1172
00:55:41,000 --> 00:55:43,000
you make a lot of mistakes.

1173
00:55:43,000 --> 00:55:44,680
And so one way that people handle

1174
00:55:44,680 --> 00:55:47,280
that is they do something called beam search, where you

1175
00:55:47,280 --> 00:55:51,140
essentially let the model choose top k like what it thinks

1176
00:55:51,140 --> 00:55:53,000
are the k most likely things.

1177
00:55:53,000 --> 00:55:55,220
And then from each of those you choose the k

1178
00:55:55,220 --> 00:55:56,680
most likely things next.

1179
00:55:56,680 --> 00:55:58,820
You fill out this entire tree, and then

1180
00:55:58,820 --> 00:56:02,757
you calculate the probability of the entire path along the tree

1181
00:56:02,757 --> 00:56:04,340
for each of them, and you pick the one

1182
00:56:04,340 --> 00:56:07,460
that scores best overall.

1183
00:56:07,460 --> 00:56:10,300
And so then the score is the model's confidence

1184
00:56:10,300 --> 00:56:13,940
of the entire sentence along that tree.

1185
00:56:13,940 --> 00:56:18,220
And there what we might learn is that actually maybe conditioned

1186
00:56:18,220 --> 00:56:22,260
on the input that this correct path on the tree

1187
00:56:22,260 --> 00:56:24,900
would score higher.

1188
00:56:24,900 --> 00:56:28,320
And this type of approach for language modeling--

1189
00:56:28,320 --> 00:56:32,940
lots of different deep things plus LSTM-flavored things put

1190
00:56:32,940 --> 00:56:37,860
together to try to generate text or describe images or do VQA--

1191
00:56:37,860 --> 00:56:40,540
this was really popular a few years ago.

1192
00:56:40,540 --> 00:56:45,340
This was definitely kind the approach that people took

1193
00:56:45,340 --> 00:56:49,760
2015-ish timeframe.

1194
00:56:49,760 --> 00:56:51,920
Of course, I think today, what we see

1195
00:56:51,920 --> 00:56:55,360
is often people will use transformers instead.

1196
00:56:55,360 --> 00:57:00,640
And we can talk a bit about what that means.

1197
00:57:00,640 --> 00:57:01,600
Yes?

1198
00:57:01,600 --> 00:57:04,600
AUDIENCE: So suppose I'm only interested in predicting

1199
00:57:04,600 --> 00:57:07,080
the third next word.

1200
00:57:07,080 --> 00:57:10,480
For example, should I do it in an autoregressive way using

1201
00:57:10,480 --> 00:57:11,780
the beam search and so on?

1202
00:57:11,780 --> 00:57:15,140
Or should I use a model that only outputs the third word?

1203
00:57:15,140 --> 00:57:17,000
Because it seems to me that there

1204
00:57:17,000 --> 00:57:20,208
is some information in the two previous words that help you.

1205
00:57:20,208 --> 00:57:22,000
SARA BEERY: I mean, I think that's probably

1206
00:57:22,000 --> 00:57:23,660
a question that would depend.

1207
00:57:23,660 --> 00:57:25,280
So the question was, what if I only

1208
00:57:25,280 --> 00:57:29,760
care about predicting the word three ahead of where I am now.

1209
00:57:29,760 --> 00:57:32,320
I don't care about the things in between.

1210
00:57:32,320 --> 00:57:34,290
Should I still model all the things

1211
00:57:34,290 --> 00:57:36,040
in between one step at a time, or should I

1212
00:57:36,040 --> 00:57:39,640
try to learn a model that predicts three words in advance?

1213
00:57:39,640 --> 00:57:41,400
I think my intuition aligns with yours

1214
00:57:41,400 --> 00:57:44,640
that there's signal in those intermediate words

1215
00:57:44,640 --> 00:57:47,790
that you would really want to propagate forward.

1216
00:57:47,790 --> 00:57:50,870
And so probably I would not try to just guess from

1217
00:57:50,870 --> 00:57:54,230
"a" what's the word three in advance without some context

1218
00:57:54,230 --> 00:57:55,730
of what the intermediate words were.

1219
00:57:55,730 --> 00:57:58,550
So I'd probably do this maybe with the beam search type thing.

1220
00:57:58,550 --> 00:58:00,630
AUDIENCE: So then if I want to predict

1221
00:58:00,630 --> 00:58:03,110
the temperature in the week, do I

1222
00:58:03,110 --> 00:58:07,922
need for every day to say [INAUDIBLE]?

1223
00:58:07,922 --> 00:58:08,630
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

1224
00:58:08,630 --> 00:58:11,390
Well, these days a lot of temperature models

1225
00:58:11,390 --> 00:58:14,687
are transformers with really large context windows.

1226
00:58:14,687 --> 00:58:16,270
So you're actually sharing information

1227
00:58:16,270 --> 00:58:19,450
across all the temperatures indeterminately.

1228
00:58:19,450 --> 00:58:22,988
And we'll talk in a second about, when you're

1229
00:58:22,988 --> 00:58:25,030
talking about these long range dependencies, what

1230
00:58:25,030 --> 00:58:27,590
the trade-offs are when you're talking about using something

1231
00:58:27,590 --> 00:58:31,430
like recursion, or even recursion with these memory

1232
00:58:31,430 --> 00:58:35,230
controllers, versus using something like attention that

1233
00:58:35,230 --> 00:58:41,590
potentially has easier ways of sharing information

1234
00:58:41,590 --> 00:58:44,130
over long time horizons-- in more flexible ways,

1235
00:58:44,130 --> 00:58:48,250
I guess is probably a better way to put it.

1236
00:58:48,250 --> 00:58:49,350
Cool.

1237
00:58:49,350 --> 00:58:53,290
So we talked previously about the fact

1238
00:58:53,290 --> 00:58:56,170
that we want to recognize that this is

1239
00:58:56,170 --> 00:58:58,370
Frank even when he's outside.

1240
00:58:58,370 --> 00:59:01,890
And just to be clear, he only gets supervised outside time,

1241
00:59:01,890 --> 00:59:04,210
because I'm a good naturalist, and I

1242
00:59:04,210 --> 00:59:07,890
don't let my cat kill birds.

1243
00:59:07,890 --> 00:59:10,470
But he gets supervised outside time on our balcony sometimes,

1244
00:59:10,470 --> 00:59:11,550
and he loves it.

1245
00:59:11,550 --> 00:59:14,330
And I want to recognize him out there.

1246
00:59:14,330 --> 00:59:20,450
So here, say we had this concept of using a memory unit.

1247
00:59:20,450 --> 00:59:24,610
So one thing you could do instead of passing memory

1248
00:59:24,610 --> 00:59:27,130
forward as you went through the sequence

1249
00:59:27,130 --> 00:59:29,530
is you could just let every single time

1250
00:59:29,530 --> 00:59:31,750
step get a memory unit.

1251
00:59:31,750 --> 00:59:36,970
You could just keep memories from every single point in time.

1252
00:59:36,970 --> 00:59:38,850
And so then you would, instead of needing

1253
00:59:38,850 --> 00:59:41,370
to figure out how to send the information forward

1254
00:59:41,370 --> 00:59:44,910
in time in this constrained way, you could just directly link

1255
00:59:44,910 --> 00:59:47,190
your old memories.

1256
00:59:47,190 --> 00:59:50,790
And so there are methods that directly link old memories

1257
00:59:50,790 --> 00:59:52,790
to future predictions.

1258
00:59:52,790 --> 00:59:56,270
Things like temporal convolutions do this.

1259
00:59:56,270 --> 00:59:58,370
Things like attention and transformers do this.

1260
00:59:58,370 --> 01:00:00,990
And we talked a lot about those last lecture.

1261
01:00:00,990 --> 01:00:03,090
And then there are also these memory networks

1262
01:00:03,090 --> 01:00:05,590
that you guys could take a look at if you're interested that

1263
01:00:05,590 --> 01:00:10,030
explicitly try to have these memory representations or units

1264
01:00:10,030 --> 01:00:12,550
that you can access over time, maybe

1265
01:00:12,550 --> 01:00:15,830
through a separate bank of memories.

1266
01:00:15,830 --> 01:00:17,990
So if we're actually trying to model arbitrarily

1267
01:00:17,990 --> 01:00:20,532
long sequences, we've talked about a couple of ways to do it.

1268
01:00:20,532 --> 01:00:23,070
So we talked about recurrence where now you

1269
01:00:23,070 --> 01:00:25,530
have these recurrent weights that are shared over time.

1270
01:00:25,530 --> 01:00:28,630
And you have this cycle of where you're passing forward

1271
01:00:28,630 --> 01:00:30,510
information over some time window.

1272
01:00:30,510 --> 01:00:32,510
And the way that you pass that information could

1273
01:00:32,510 --> 01:00:34,050
be just vanilla recursion.

1274
01:00:34,050 --> 01:00:35,330
It be something like an LSTM.

1275
01:00:35,330 --> 01:00:37,630
There's a lot more complicated ways to do it.

1276
01:00:37,630 --> 01:00:39,370
We talked about convolution.

1277
01:00:39,370 --> 01:00:41,810
You can model an arbitrarily long sequence of convolution.

1278
01:00:41,810 --> 01:00:44,432
But now you have this fixed set of convolution weights.

1279
01:00:44,432 --> 01:00:45,890
Those are shared over time, but you

1280
01:00:45,890 --> 01:00:48,290
don't get to see anything outside of the time

1281
01:00:48,290 --> 01:00:50,730
window of that convolution.

1282
01:00:50,730 --> 01:00:53,370
And then the last thing we talk about is attention where, here,

1283
01:00:53,370 --> 01:00:54,970
weights are dynamically determined

1284
01:00:54,970 --> 01:00:56,750
as a function of the data.

1285
01:00:56,750 --> 01:01:01,170
So you have this convolutional kernel with attention weights.

1286
01:01:01,170 --> 01:01:04,470
So if you look at recurrence, that looks like this.

1287
01:01:04,470 --> 01:01:06,330
You're passing that forward in time.

1288
01:01:06,330 --> 01:01:07,772
Convolution looks like this.

1289
01:01:07,772 --> 01:01:09,230
So you're getting that time window,

1290
01:01:09,230 --> 01:01:11,090
but you're not passing it forward in time.

1291
01:01:11,090 --> 01:01:13,010
And then attention will look something

1292
01:01:13,010 --> 01:01:18,330
like this, where now the attention weights are

1293
01:01:18,330 --> 01:01:19,470
a function of the data.

1294
01:01:19,470 --> 01:01:22,210
So the way that you keep the information

1295
01:01:22,210 --> 01:01:26,690
is dependent on the inputs, which is quite flexible.

1296
01:01:26,690 --> 01:01:29,390
And then, of course, in all of these cases,

1297
01:01:29,390 --> 01:01:31,650
you can have some sense of, at least the bottom two,

1298
01:01:31,650 --> 01:01:33,532
of what this time window is.

1299
01:01:33,532 --> 01:01:35,490
And the top one, we do talk about a time window

1300
01:01:35,490 --> 01:01:37,570
for backprop.

1301
01:01:37,570 --> 01:01:40,383
So this is interesting, because now,

1302
01:01:40,383 --> 01:01:42,050
if you take these different approaches--

1303
01:01:42,050 --> 01:01:44,750
if you use something like self-attention, or recurrence,

1304
01:01:44,750 --> 01:01:46,550
or convolution--

1305
01:01:46,550 --> 01:01:52,990
they're going to be different computational complexities based

1306
01:01:52,990 --> 01:01:56,830
on the sequence length-- so how big your sequence is--

1307
01:01:56,830 --> 01:01:59,347
the dimension of the representation,

1308
01:01:59,347 --> 01:02:01,430
and then like the kernel size of your convolution,

1309
01:02:01,430 --> 01:02:02,930
and the size of the neighborhood,

1310
01:02:02,930 --> 01:02:04,550
I guess, if you're going to have some of restricted

1311
01:02:04,550 --> 01:02:06,383
self-attention, like a window that's sliding

1312
01:02:06,383 --> 01:02:08,990
instead of just self-attention over the entire string.

1313
01:02:08,990 --> 01:02:11,470
And this is actually from that "Attention is All you Need"

1314
01:02:11,470 --> 01:02:11,850
paper.

1315
01:02:11,850 --> 01:02:13,517
And the name "Attention is All you Need"

1316
01:02:13,517 --> 01:02:17,530
is actually really trying to say that we don't need memory.

1317
01:02:17,530 --> 01:02:19,750
All we need is attention.

1318
01:02:19,750 --> 01:02:21,870
And here, you can see that when you

1319
01:02:21,870 --> 01:02:23,370
talk about the number of operations,

1320
01:02:23,370 --> 01:02:25,210
the maximal length, the complexity,

1321
01:02:25,210 --> 01:02:28,510
there's a lot of trade-offs here in terms of what's possible.

1322
01:02:28,510 --> 01:02:30,630
But of course, with something like self-attention,

1323
01:02:30,630 --> 01:02:32,030
you have this n squared.

1324
01:02:32,030 --> 01:02:34,350
In terms of the length of the sequence,

1325
01:02:34,350 --> 01:02:36,250
in terms of the complexity per layer,

1326
01:02:36,250 --> 01:02:38,190
because you have to calculate the similarity

1327
01:02:38,190 --> 01:02:39,840
of the entire sequence to itself.

1328
01:02:39,840 --> 01:02:41,340
So if your sequence is length n, you

1329
01:02:41,340 --> 01:02:44,220
need to calculate this n squared computation.

1330
01:02:44,220 --> 01:02:47,580
And you can imagine that that can get really computationally

1331
01:02:47,580 --> 01:02:48,200
exhaustive.

1332
01:02:48,200 --> 01:02:51,923
It takes a lot of memory to store something like that large.

1333
01:02:51,923 --> 01:02:53,340
So there's a bunch of methods that

1334
01:02:53,340 --> 01:02:55,980
have been developed in recent years that try to figure out

1335
01:02:55,980 --> 01:03:01,700
how to make even larger context window transformers given

1336
01:03:01,700 --> 01:03:02,980
limited memory.

1337
01:03:02,980 --> 01:03:07,720
And so these are things like efficiency from sparsification,

1338
01:03:07,720 --> 01:03:12,140
so things like the Reformer, or Performers, or Linformers

1339
01:03:12,140 --> 01:03:14,880
that are all different ways to try to decompose attention--

1340
01:03:14,880 --> 01:03:17,880
do something like a low matrix approximation--

1341
01:03:17,880 --> 01:03:21,500
a low rank matrix approximation to get that context window

1342
01:03:21,500 --> 01:03:25,180
computation from big O of n squared to big O of n.

1343
01:03:25,180 --> 01:03:29,100
Or things like the Reformer uses this quadratic dot product

1344
01:03:29,100 --> 01:03:33,780
attention that uses local hashing to get it to n log n.

1345
01:03:33,780 --> 01:03:38,240
And this is definitely something that people are quite interested

1346
01:03:38,240 --> 01:03:38,740
in.

1347
01:03:38,740 --> 01:03:42,440
But also frequently, this comes at the expense

1348
01:03:42,440 --> 01:03:43,920
of a little bit of performance.

1349
01:03:43,920 --> 01:03:46,560
And so if the trade off is performance

1350
01:03:46,560 --> 01:03:49,680
versus computational efficiency, we just

1351
01:03:49,680 --> 01:03:53,560
build GPUs with more memory.

1352
01:03:53,560 --> 01:03:55,760
And then there's other ways that people try and look

1353
01:03:55,760 --> 01:03:58,000
at even larger context transformers.

1354
01:03:58,000 --> 01:03:59,300
So instead of-- yes?

1355
01:03:59,300 --> 01:04:02,100
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] less performant but more efficient,

1356
01:04:02,100 --> 01:04:04,480
can't you just use the extra time

1357
01:04:04,480 --> 01:04:06,520
that you gain to train it [INAUDIBLE]

1358
01:04:06,520 --> 01:04:08,840
and get a better prediction?

1359
01:04:08,840 --> 01:04:12,160
SARA BEERY: So the question is, if it's less performant but more

1360
01:04:12,160 --> 01:04:14,680
efficient, could you use the time on the GPU

1361
01:04:14,680 --> 01:04:16,158
to train on more data?

1362
01:04:16,158 --> 01:04:18,700
I don't think that's actually the choice that they're making.

1363
01:04:18,700 --> 01:04:20,825
I think they're going to train on all the data they

1364
01:04:20,825 --> 01:04:25,560
have with the biggest thing they have.

1365
01:04:25,560 --> 01:04:27,000
[COUGHS]

1366
01:04:27,000 --> 01:04:29,578
Sorry, guys.

1367
01:04:29,578 --> 01:04:31,120
So another mechanism that people have

1368
01:04:31,120 --> 01:04:34,800
used to try to increase the context window of transformers

1369
01:04:34,800 --> 01:04:39,060
is this idea of local and global attention.

1370
01:04:39,060 --> 01:04:42,743
So things like Transformer XL uses segment-level recurrence

1371
01:04:42,743 --> 01:04:44,660
and then these very fancy positional encodings

1372
01:04:44,660 --> 01:04:46,740
to increase the context window.

1373
01:04:46,740 --> 01:04:49,900
Longformers scales with sequence length

1374
01:04:49,900 --> 01:04:53,140
by deconstructing local and global attention.

1375
01:04:53,140 --> 01:04:54,780
[COUGHS]

1376
01:04:54,780 --> 01:04:56,540
Sorry.

1377
01:04:56,540 --> 01:05:00,177
This is from Longformer, I believe, this figure

1378
01:05:00,177 --> 01:05:00,760
at the bottom.

1379
01:05:00,760 --> 01:05:03,740
So what you can see is that they're basically sparsifying

1380
01:05:03,740 --> 01:05:05,640
that n by n computation.

1381
01:05:05,640 --> 01:05:09,240
So the previous one was sparsifying via decomposition.

1382
01:05:09,240 --> 01:05:12,070
This one is sparsifying via some of masking.

1383
01:05:12,070 --> 01:05:13,820
But essentially, ways to try to figure out

1384
01:05:13,820 --> 01:05:17,120
how you don't need to do n times n computations.

1385
01:05:20,830 --> 01:05:22,580
And then another thing that was introduced

1386
01:05:22,580 --> 01:05:24,740
to try to increase the context of transformers

1387
01:05:24,740 --> 01:05:29,460
without needing to scale memory with n squared--

1388
01:05:29,460 --> 01:05:33,820
was this idea that maybe something like a large GPT that

1389
01:05:33,820 --> 01:05:37,640
will jointly model all the world's language and all

1390
01:05:37,640 --> 01:05:41,360
the world's information could be broken down or decomposed

1391
01:05:41,360 --> 01:05:45,200
into something that models just language, that's much more

1392
01:05:45,200 --> 01:05:48,560
lightweight, with some of lookup table or memory

1393
01:05:48,560 --> 01:05:54,720
bank that is able to let us do fact lookup in a database.

1394
01:05:54,720 --> 01:05:57,200
And the other benefit of something like this

1395
01:05:57,200 --> 01:06:04,020
is there's ways to, again, ensure factual validity.

1396
01:06:04,020 --> 01:06:07,280
Because here, we're predicting what's likely,

1397
01:06:07,280 --> 01:06:09,373
and it's learned based on the world's information.

1398
01:06:09,373 --> 01:06:11,040
But you can't guarantee that it actually

1399
01:06:11,040 --> 01:06:12,980
matches the truth of the world's information.

1400
01:06:12,980 --> 01:06:15,040
Whereas if you keep the knowledge base,

1401
01:06:15,040 --> 01:06:18,180
this kind of lookup of facts separate,

1402
01:06:18,180 --> 01:06:21,640
then you can try to enforce that those facts are actually

1403
01:06:21,640 --> 01:06:24,460
going to be true, that you won't have things like hallucinations.

1404
01:06:24,460 --> 01:06:28,520
Of course, there's still some complexity there.

1405
01:06:28,520 --> 01:06:31,440
And so this is like retrieval enhanced transformers,

1406
01:06:31,440 --> 01:06:32,440
essentially--

1407
01:06:32,440 --> 01:06:34,480
so things like this paper called retro.

1408
01:06:34,480 --> 01:06:36,430
And this gives you this trillion token

1409
01:06:36,430 --> 01:06:38,110
database that just gets stored in memory

1410
01:06:38,110 --> 01:06:41,870
and gets accessed by your language model.

1411
01:06:41,870 --> 01:06:42,950
Cool.

1412
01:06:42,950 --> 01:06:44,690
So how far back can we go though?

1413
01:06:44,690 --> 01:06:46,050
Is this actually a limitation?

1414
01:06:46,050 --> 01:06:48,190
Do we need to decrease our context window

1415
01:06:48,190 --> 01:06:49,610
for something like attention?

1416
01:06:49,610 --> 01:06:51,010
How much memory do we need.

1417
01:06:51,010 --> 01:06:52,552
And it kind of gets to this question,

1418
01:06:52,552 --> 01:06:55,910
do we really care about modeling things

1419
01:06:55,910 --> 01:06:58,470
at the beginning of Pride and Prejudice by the time

1420
01:06:58,470 --> 01:07:00,490
you get to the last chapter?

1421
01:07:00,490 --> 01:07:02,825
Do we care?

1422
01:07:02,825 --> 01:07:05,450
And if you look at the attention window of some of these models

1423
01:07:05,450 --> 01:07:10,950
as they've kind of scaled, BERT had 512 token attention--

1424
01:07:10,950 --> 01:07:12,830
or context window.

1425
01:07:12,830 --> 01:07:14,950
GPT-2 had 1,024.

1426
01:07:14,950 --> 01:07:17,470
GPT-3 was 2,048.

1427
01:07:17,470 --> 01:07:20,990
GPT-4 was, by default, 8,000, but they had a souped up version

1428
01:07:20,990 --> 01:07:23,990
that could do 32,000.

1429
01:07:23,990 --> 01:07:27,290
Anthropic has a model that has about 100K,

1430
01:07:27,290 --> 01:07:28,850
so it's like 75,000 words.

1431
01:07:28,850 --> 01:07:32,150
That's like two or three books worth of context.

1432
01:07:32,150 --> 01:07:36,370
So if we can fit this in memory, do we need anything else?

1433
01:07:36,370 --> 01:07:43,370
Well, one way that we might is how many people can afford H100s

1434
01:07:43,370 --> 01:07:47,450
or whatever the best and brightest version of a GPU is?

1435
01:07:47,450 --> 01:07:50,090
So from my perspective, I think figuring out

1436
01:07:50,090 --> 01:07:52,250
when we actually need this much context,

1437
01:07:52,250 --> 01:07:55,170
and how we should use it, and in what ways

1438
01:07:55,170 --> 01:07:59,410
is valuable from the sense of efficiency and accessibility

1439
01:07:59,410 --> 01:08:00,970
of these models.

1440
01:08:00,970 --> 01:08:05,530
And this takes a lot of power, and it takes a lot of energy,

1441
01:08:05,530 --> 01:08:07,210
and it's really slow to train.

1442
01:08:07,210 --> 01:08:10,450
And so I think efficiency gains on in terms

1443
01:08:10,450 --> 01:08:12,850
of how well we model time and how

1444
01:08:12,850 --> 01:08:15,250
we think about what's needed to model in time, I think,

1445
01:08:15,250 --> 01:08:16,609
are really valuable.

1446
01:08:16,609 --> 01:08:19,130
And there's a nice paper-- it's maybe two years old now

1447
01:08:19,130 --> 01:08:20,850
from Jitendra Malik's group-- that

1448
01:08:20,850 --> 01:08:23,990
talks about when do we actually need long-term context?

1449
01:08:23,990 --> 01:08:25,970
And they do this within the frame of reference

1450
01:08:25,970 --> 01:08:28,470
of video action recognition.

1451
01:08:28,470 --> 01:08:31,490
So if you have a video, how much temporal information

1452
01:08:31,490 --> 01:08:34,470
do you actually need to recognize the action?

1453
01:08:34,470 --> 01:08:36,310
And what they were pointing out--

1454
01:08:36,310 --> 01:08:39,510
so they introduced this idea of a minimum certificate set, which

1455
01:08:39,510 --> 01:08:43,069
is essentially the total amount of frames

1456
01:08:43,069 --> 01:08:46,590
or little clips of a video that you need in terms of time

1457
01:08:46,590 --> 01:08:49,229
to recognize an action accurately.

1458
01:08:49,229 --> 01:08:51,870
And so here, if you have this entire video,

1459
01:08:51,870 --> 01:08:54,790
and you want to recognize what's going on in the video,

1460
01:08:54,790 --> 01:08:58,390
basically, how many of those frames of the video are needed?

1461
01:08:58,390 --> 01:09:02,229
And that's the amount of context you really need.

1462
01:09:02,229 --> 01:09:07,029
And what they showed was that most of the video action

1463
01:09:07,029 --> 01:09:08,630
recognition benchmarks we actually

1464
01:09:08,630 --> 01:09:13,270
study needed somewhere up to maybe like

1465
01:09:13,270 --> 01:09:15,310
two seconds of context.

1466
01:09:15,310 --> 01:09:19,850
We didn't need models that could handle three-hour long movies,

1467
01:09:19,850 --> 01:09:23,029
because actually, the actions we cared about in our benchmarks

1468
01:09:23,029 --> 01:09:25,390
were like two or three seconds.

1469
01:09:25,390 --> 01:09:27,550
And so their point was that what we need

1470
01:09:27,550 --> 01:09:30,710
is we need to develop benchmarks for things that actually need

1471
01:09:30,710 --> 01:09:34,090
long term context in order to encourage the community to do

1472
01:09:34,090 --> 01:09:36,330
work that shows benefit there.

1473
01:09:36,330 --> 01:09:38,090
Because it's really hard for us to measure

1474
01:09:38,090 --> 01:09:41,290
progress on something that we're just not measuring.

1475
01:09:41,290 --> 01:09:44,970
And so they introduced some new EgoSchema benchmark

1476
01:09:44,970 --> 01:09:47,887
that had certificate lengths of more like 100.

1477
01:09:47,887 --> 01:09:49,970
But I think that this is an interesting point just

1478
01:09:49,970 --> 01:09:51,029
from a meta perspective.

1479
01:09:51,029 --> 01:09:55,230
It kind of tells us about how the benchmarks that we build,

1480
01:09:55,230 --> 01:09:58,810
the ways that we model value, we model improvement in the machine

1481
01:09:58,810 --> 01:10:02,490
learning space directly influence what we make progress

1482
01:10:02,490 --> 01:10:04,648
on, because we want to optimize these benchmarks.

1483
01:10:04,648 --> 01:10:07,190
And so if there's something that our benchmarks are missing--

1484
01:10:07,190 --> 01:10:08,490
if there are gaps--

1485
01:10:08,490 --> 01:10:10,910
that means that we don't know what we don't know.

1486
01:10:10,910 --> 01:10:12,477
And so we might say, oh, well, we

1487
01:10:12,477 --> 01:10:14,810
want to build these models that have really long context

1488
01:10:14,810 --> 01:10:15,310
windows.

1489
01:10:15,310 --> 01:10:18,130
But then they're just-- the more context windows we have,

1490
01:10:18,130 --> 01:10:19,790
or the more temporal context we have,

1491
01:10:19,790 --> 01:10:21,590
it's not really improving performance.

1492
01:10:21,590 --> 01:10:24,090
Maybe it's because the benchmark we're measuring performance

1493
01:10:24,090 --> 01:10:29,290
on just doesn't actually include anything that needs it.

1494
01:10:29,290 --> 01:10:32,070
And then finally, I want to talk a little bit

1495
01:10:32,070 --> 01:10:34,510
about just this different perspective

1496
01:10:34,510 --> 01:10:37,510
on fast and slow memory.

1497
01:10:37,510 --> 01:10:40,258
So here's one interesting thing.

1498
01:10:40,258 --> 01:10:42,550
If you think about a neural network as something that's

1499
01:10:42,550 --> 01:10:48,270
frozen, and you send data into it, then the activations

1500
01:10:48,270 --> 01:10:50,890
that you get through that neural network,

1501
01:10:50,890 --> 01:10:53,050
there's some statistic of that data point.

1502
01:10:53,050 --> 01:10:55,810
You can think of them as like fast memory.

1503
01:10:58,950 --> 01:11:01,990
So they're actually building you some representation

1504
01:11:01,990 --> 01:11:05,750
that's very fast, that's kind of capturing memories of how

1505
01:11:05,750 --> 01:11:08,030
that data is running through the network.

1506
01:11:08,030 --> 01:11:11,190
Similarly, if you're learning the parameters

1507
01:11:11,190 --> 01:11:14,110
of a network via training from a large data set,

1508
01:11:14,110 --> 01:11:18,350
you can think of the theta, the actual parameter

1509
01:11:18,350 --> 01:11:21,910
values in that network as representing some statistics

1510
01:11:21,910 --> 01:11:23,130
of that training data.

1511
01:11:23,130 --> 01:11:26,350
So in a way, those parameters in a deep net

1512
01:11:26,350 --> 01:11:30,580
are slow memory of the training data.

1513
01:11:30,580 --> 01:11:31,760
And this is interesting.

1514
01:11:31,760 --> 01:11:35,640
So we can think of parameters are maybe slow memory.

1515
01:11:35,640 --> 01:11:38,740
You're slowly building this memory of the entire training

1516
01:11:38,740 --> 01:11:39,740
data set.

1517
01:11:39,740 --> 01:11:41,480
And activations are fast memory.

1518
01:11:41,480 --> 01:11:45,900
It's some statistics or some memory of some existing data

1519
01:11:45,900 --> 01:11:47,140
point.

1520
01:11:47,140 --> 01:11:51,100
But do we ever think about fast memory weights of a model,

1521
01:11:51,100 --> 01:11:54,340
or slow memory activations of a model?

1522
01:11:54,340 --> 01:11:58,660
And just as something to give you food for thought,

1523
01:11:58,660 --> 01:12:01,960
there is this idea of hypernets that were released,

1524
01:12:01,960 --> 01:12:04,200
which are nets that output weights of other nets.

1525
01:12:04,200 --> 01:12:08,300
They're used as a version of neural architecture search.

1526
01:12:08,300 --> 01:12:12,100
And so those weights are almost like a fast memory of the input

1527
01:12:12,100 --> 01:12:13,280
data to that net.

1528
01:12:13,280 --> 01:12:16,660
You're getting as the output of your network

1529
01:12:16,660 --> 01:12:18,900
another network that's a set of weights.

1530
01:12:18,900 --> 01:12:21,420
Or there are these ideas of codebooks.

1531
01:12:21,420 --> 01:12:25,580
These actually use tensors of activations that are learned.

1532
01:12:25,580 --> 01:12:28,610
So you're doing backprop to the activations themselves.

1533
01:12:28,610 --> 01:12:30,360
And you could argue that these activations

1534
01:12:30,360 --> 01:12:35,800
are kind of slow memory of the data set you're learning.

1535
01:12:35,800 --> 01:12:39,880
So I think there's ways to flip the perspective a little bit

1536
01:12:39,880 --> 01:12:44,240
and try to think about how we have

1537
01:12:44,240 --> 01:12:50,080
memory that's stored in the weights, in the activations.

1538
01:12:50,080 --> 01:12:52,683
What are the ways that we're representing

1539
01:12:52,683 --> 01:12:54,600
what the data we're learning or the data we're

1540
01:12:54,600 --> 01:12:59,040
actually using at any given point in time.

1541
01:12:59,040 --> 01:13:00,020
Cool.

1542
01:13:00,020 --> 01:13:02,160
Any other questions?

1543
01:13:02,160 --> 01:13:07,200
Otherwise, I'll just leave you with another picture of Frank,

1544
01:13:07,200 --> 01:13:08,460
who's the best cat ever.

1545
01:13:08,460 --> 01:13:11,816
[APPLAUSE]

1546
01:13:13,580 --> 01:13:14,080
